//! beam-zig M7 / S17+S18+S20: **processes, signals, monitors/links** and the
//! VM that interprets the machine's effect traps (cf. erts erl_process.c,
//! erl_proc_sig_queue.c, erl_monitor_link.c).
//!
//! The modern erts insight, adopted whole: EVERYTHING between processes is a
//! signal — messages, exits, monitor downs, link/unlink requests. The one
//! sacred law (erts documents it as such):
//!
//!   SIGNAL ORDER: signals from process A to process B are delivered in the
//!   order A sent them, for every ordered pair (A, B) — across ALL kinds.
//!
//! Everything else layers on it: mailbox FIFO-per-pair (M5) is its message
//! projection; monitor/link semantics are trace properties over it.
//!
//! Machine ↔ VM boundary (doctrine: effects at the edge): the M3/M5 machine
//! stays pure — cross-process instructions trap into a `pending: Action`;
//! the VM interprets the action and resumes the machine. Message payloads
//! cross heaps via gcCopy — the M1 copy law ("copy preserves denotation")
//! is re-proven here at the send boundary, as the cross-cutting register
//! demands.
//!
//! Laws (this file):
//!   per-pair signal order (the sacred one), over random workloads & engines
//!   send copies preserve denotation (cross-heap)
//!   link symmetry; exit propagation respects trap_exit ('EXIT' tuples);
//!     normal exits only kill the exiting process
//!   'DOWN' delivered exactly once per monitor ref; demonitor ⇒ no 'DOWN'
//!   E5.5 alias-signal model: a send to an ACTIVE alias ref lands in the owner's
//!     mailbox; unalias / owner-death / monitor-'DOWN' (non-explicit_unalias)
//!     retire it so later sends drop; is_process_alive/2 replies {Ref, bool}
//!   E5.8 (Task 8, DIVERGENCE 50): registered-NAME send (`Name ! Msg` routes to
//!     the pid the name is bound to; an unregistered name drops); spawn_monitor
//!     ([monitor] spawn returns {Pid,Ref} + installs a monitor whose child-exit
//!     'DOWN' fires — spawn_monitor is an erlang.erl library wrapper over
//!     spawn_opt(_,_,_,[monitor]), expanded inline by the loader, ledger-invisible)
//!   scheduler: oracle (round-robin) vs seeded engine — per-process event
//!     order preserved, per-pair delivery order preserved, no starvation,
//!     and FULL result equality on single-sender topologies (G3 programs)
//!   G3 gate: token ring + scatter/gather with selective receive
//!   E6.1 SMP (S17, `SmpEngine`): N per-scheduler run queues + work stealing,
//!     admitted ONLY by equivalence to the sequential ORACLE — scheduler-count
//!     invariance homomorphism (denote(P,1)==denote(P,N)), work-stealing
//!     conservation (a pid is runnable on ≤1 scheduler), the SACRED per-pair
//!     order re-proven under seeded multi-scheduler stress, a monitor
//!     linearizability spot-law, and BOUNDED JOINS over real `std.Thread`
//!     workers behind a coarse VM-lock seam. THE KEY INSIGHT: all signalling
//!     flows through the single stamping authority `Vm.signal`, so the scheduler
//!     decides only WHEN a process runs, never a pair's delivery order — M7
//!     survives SMP by construction (SAFETY §7w / CA-20).
//!   E6.4 SUSPENSION (S17, `Vm.suspend_a[pid]` + `schedulable`, gap-b-6a): the process-
//!     suspension family — `erts_internal:suspend_process/2` + `erlang:resume_process/1`
//!     are the counting MONOID over the `L_c`-domain per-pid `suspend_a` (nested suspends
//!     stack, matching resumes unwind them, resume past the unit → `badarg`). The
//!     count is ORTHOGONAL to `machine.status`: `schedulable` (the single predicate
//!     every run-queue/discover/migrate/quiescence site consults) grants CPU only
//!     when the count is 0, so a suspended process is skipped by the sequential
//!     ORACLE and every SMP engine alike. `is_system_process/1` reads a target's
//!     `system` flag; `process_info(P,status)` reports `suspended` when the count
//!     is > 0. Proven by the monoid/scheduler-skip/totality laws + the `suspend_fam`
//!     corpus differential (DIVERGENCE 112).
//!   E6.6 DIRTY POOLS (S17, `SmpEngine.driveModelDirty`): dirty CPU/IO schedulers
//!     are EXTRA run queues — a dirty-tagged process migrates OFF a normal
//!     scheduler onto a dirty pool, is granted there IDENTICALLY, and migrates
//!     BACK on the dirty BIF's return. The grant is the same wherever a process
//!     runs, so the DIRTY-MIGRATION HOMOMORPHISM holds (`denote(dirty)==denote(
//!     normal)` over the confluent corpus) and the SACRED per-pair order survives
//!     a dirty round-trip (all signalling still flows through `Vm.signal` — the
//!     entry-100 insight; a signal to a dirty-parked process is delivered on its
//!     return). Conservation is measured across BOTH pools. The dirty-tagged pure
//!     BIF rows (insert/delete_element, float<->binary, display) ride ordinary
//!     differentials; the pool is a scheduling hint, never a denotation change
//!     (SAFETY §7y / CA-22, DIVERGENCE 120).
//!   E6.9 SCALABILITY WORKLOAD (S17, `scaleWork`/`scale_worker`): the throughput
//!     subject the conformance harness measures at n∈{1,2,4} schedulers
//!     (OTP30_PARITY_PLAN.md §2.3). A spawn-storm of INDEPENDENT bounded workers
//!     driven under `SmpEngine.driveModel`; the completed-work count (grants) is
//!     a pure function of (n_workers, program length, fuel) — INVARIANT of the
//!     scheduler count. That `work(n)==work(1)` law is what makes the harness's
//!     throughput ratio s(n)=throughput(n)/throughput(1) meaningful: only
//!     wall-time varies with n, never the work. Recorded via `zigvm scale`; the
//!     evidence path is SAFETY CA-23 / SC-23.1 (record scale measurement).
//!   E7.5 PROCESS-ENGINE COMPLETIONS (DIVERGENCE 154): (a) `hibernate/3`
//!     (`doHibernate`) — DISCARD the calling process's call stack and RE-ENTER
//!     at `M:F/A` when the next message arrives (the MFA-reentry homomorphism;
//!     the mailbox is preserved, the re-entered `receive` finds queued messages).
//!     A LOCAL control-flow reset — no signal, so the SACRED M7 order holds (the
//!     `run_on_load` precedent). Flips EQ (the `phibernate` corpus). (b) the async
//!     SYSTEM-TASK QUEUE (`doRequestSystemTask`/`deliverParkedSysTask`, the E5.6
//!     parked-reply sibling) — `request_system_task/3,4` parks a `{RequestId,
//!     Result}` reply to the requester, flushed on the next tick over `Vm.signal`
//!     (garbage_collect → `true`); law-proven exactly-once, but the request
//!     ENCODING is a 28-vs-30 skew so the row stays `deferred-E7-systaskq`. (c)
//!     `process_info` gains two byte-total non-pid items (error_handler,
//!     message_queue_data); the residual (impl-counters + pid-values) is unmoved.
//!   E8.2v DIST REMOTE EXIT OBSERVER (S27): foreign-pid `exit_signal/2,3`
//!     leaves the BIF layer as `.remote_exit_signal` and the Vm records an
//!     outbound remote-exit frame intent only when a live `DistEntry` matches
//!     the pid's node and creation. No-live, pending-only, or stale-creation
//!     rows remain inert `true`; actual socket/TCP serialization is still the
//!     carrier owner's work.
//!   E8.2w DIST DISCONNECT QUEUE BOUNDARY (S27): a live carrier disconnect
//!     clears every queued outbound remote-exit intent for that peer before the
//!     row becomes non-live. Unrelated peers remain queued in order; after
//!     disconnect, new foreign-pid exits for that node are inert until a fresh
//!     live entry is promoted. Monitor `{nodedown,Node}` delivery is unchanged.
//!   E8.2x DIST SOCKET DISCONNECT ACTION BRIDGE (S27/CA-19): a carrier-origin
//!     disconnect enters the Vm through a single `.disconnect_dist_node` pending
//!     action. The action reuses the same live-row retirement, remote-exit queue
//!     cleanup, and monitor notification boundary as internal disconnect; pending
//!     or absent rows return `false` and fabricate no `{nodedown,Node}` signal.
//!     This is the control-action doorway, not a real TCP carrier.
//!   E8.2y DIST REMOTE EXIT WIRE INTENT (S27/CA-19): each outbound remote-exit
//!     intent owns the ETF bytes for its reason at append time. A future carrier
//!     can drain the queue without reading the sender heap; disconnect cleanup
//!     frees those bytes with the peer-scoped queue row.
//!   E8.2z DIST PEER DFLAG REFRESH (S27/CA-19): an in-memory carrier may refresh
//!     the stored peer DFLAGS for an already-live `DistEntry`, but only when the
//!     node and peer creation match. It creates no handle, socket, or process.
//!   E8.2ac/E8.2ad DIST MANDATORY DFLAGS (S27/CA-19): live channel promotion
//!     accepts optional peer capability loss but rejects peers missing the
//!     pinned `erts_dflags.mandatory` bitset, keeping unadmitted rows pending
//!     and invisible; strict-order is a separate tuple field, not mandatory.
//!
//! Scope notes: the M7 engine + law suite name processes by SMALL-INT pids
//! (ring/G3 programs put an int in x14 and send to int targets); E3.7 makes
//! `pidOfTerm` TOTAL over BOTH those and the real pid TERMs (Task 5) that the
//! process bridge (`bifs/procsys.zig`) uses — a pid term's `number` field IS the
//! proc index (the law-covered pid↔process mapping). External/remote pids stay
//! E5. Receive has TWO models: the M5/M7 `recv_*` polling ops (fuel-burning,
//! never `.suspended`) AND the E1.11 `loop_rec`/`wait` suspend model — E3.7 adds
//! the RECEIVE-WAKE so a `.suspended` process resumes when a signal is enqueued
//! (`Vm.signal` sets it `.running`), upgrading DIVERGENCE entry 5's terminal-
//! `wait` for the multi-process case; the real receive TIMER stays E6. The whole
//! scheduler run is fuel/slice-BOUNDED — a hang is a failed law, never a stuck
//! suite (all-suspended ⇒ quiescence). `cli.run` (E3.7) drives this scheduler.

const std = @import("std");
const ta = @import("term_algebra.zig");
const ia = @import("instr_algebra.zig");
const mba = @import("mailbox_algebra.zig");
const procsys = @import("bifs/procsys.zig"); // E3.7: the process-bridge BIFs
const conv = @import("bifs/conv.zig"); // E8.2u: Machine-aware term_to_binary/external_size observer
const etf = @import("etf.zig"); // E8.2u law: pure encoder remains term-payload-owned
const io_bifs = @import("bifs/io.zig"); // E4.6: the io:* surface (put_chars/nl)
const ets_bifs = @import("bifs/ets.zig"); // E6.7: resolveTable for ets:give_away/3
const ea = @import("ets_algebra.zig"); // E7.3: the Vm-owned shared ETS registry
const registry = @import("registry.zig"); // E3.8: the registry name<->pid bijection
const msc = @import("matchspec.zig"); // gap-tracing-firepath: trace-body match-spec compile/run at the call-trace fire site
const os_port = @import("os_port.zig"); // E5.4: the live os-port driver (Stratum C)
const port_algebra = @import("port_algebra.zig"); // gap-open-port-line: {line,N} framing (lineFrames)
const socket_algebra = @import("socket_algebra.zig"); // gap-socket-gen-tcp-bifs: real gen_tcp/gen_udp transport (Stratum C)
const socket_bifs = @import("bifs/socket.zig"); // gap-socket-real-tcp: gen_tcp/inet name-dispatch BifFns
const os_bif = @import("bifs/os.zig"); // gap-os-signals-delivery: the pending-signal set the handler writes
const bif_dispatch = @import("bifs/dispatch.zig"); // gap-dynamic-native-dispatch: resolveDynamic for runtime M:F/A
const erlang_bifs = @import("bifs/erlang.zig"); // M1: decode_packet/3 (HTTP request parse)
const application_bifs = @import("bifs/application.zig"); // gap-app-boot-engine: application:get_env / code:ensure_loaded
const fun_info_bifs = @import("bifs/fun_info.zig"); // gap-app-boot-engine: function_exported/3 (dyn-aware)
const timer_wheel = @import("timer_wheel.zig"); // E6.2: the LIVE receive/BIF timer wheel
const smp_trace = @import("smp_trace.zig"); // S-epoch SMP-OBS: lock contention + order-checker hooks (zero-cost when smp_trace.enabled == false)
const prim_file = @import("prim_file.zig"); // gap-fs-autoloader: the bounded real-fd file seam
const gc_mod = @import("gc.zig"); // e48: hibernate's memory-minimize half (the real collector)
const code_bifs = @import("bifs/code.zig"); // gap-fs-autoloader: loadBeamBytes (stage+commit fused)
const dispatch = @import("dispatch.zig"); // jit-wire-production: engine #4 on the real per-process run path

const AtomTable = ta.AtomTable;
const FinalTerms = ta.FinalTerms;

/// gap-r2c-behavioral-parity (DIVERGENCE 607, Inc1): the reduction-preempt instr
/// FAIL-SAFE multiplier — when preempting on a reduction budget F, a 0-reduction
/// straight-line hog is force-yielded after `F * this` INSTRUCTIONS (UCA-R2C-1). Real
/// BEAM has no 0-reduction loops (every loop is a reduction-charging tail-call), so the
/// cap NEVER fires for real code; it exists only to bound a pathological synthetic hog.
/// Large enough that any program charging ≥1 reduction per this-many instrs preempts on
/// reductions first (behavioral parity), never on the cap.
const REDUCTION_PREEMPT_INSTR_CAP_MULT: u64 = 1024;
const spec = ta.spec;
const LawConfig = ta.LawConfig;
const expectLaw = ta.expectLaw;

pub const Pid = u32;

/// A signal, in erts's unified sense.
/// e48-smp-s2: a HEAP FRAGMENT — the OTP-style send carrier. The SENDER copies
/// its message into a fresh fragment heap (never touching the receiver's ctx);
/// the RECEIVER merges the fragment into its own heap when it drains the
/// signal (then the fragment is freed). This severs the sender→receiver-heap
/// write dependency — the S3 precondition for granting two processes on two
/// real threads (a sender must not mutate a heap another thread is running).
pub const Fragment = struct { ctx: FinalTerms.Ctx, root: FinalTerms.Term, prio: bool = false };

/// gap-tracing-e7: a GLOBAL call-trace breakpoint predicate installed by
/// `erlang:trace_pattern/2,3`. A `(module, func, arity)` triple where `null` is
/// the `'_'` wildcard. The set of ENABLED predicates gates whether a `call`-traced
/// process's MFA dispatch delivers a `{trace,Pid,call,{M,F,Args}}` message. NOTE
/// (honest scope): match-spec BODIES are NOT executed — `true`/`[]`/a compiled
/// list all just ENABLE the breakpoint and `false` clears it; compiled match-spec
/// filtering/`{return_trace}`/`meta`-tracer/session semantics stay deferred.
pub const TracePattern = struct {
    module: ?ta.AtomIdx,
    func: ?ta.AtomIdx,
    arity: ?u32,
    // gap-tracing-firepath: the installed match spec, deep-copied (gcCopy) into
    // the Vm's stable `trace_spec_region` so it survives caller GC. `null` = a
    // `true`/`[]`/beyond-subset spec (enable-only; the call trace delivers bare).
    spec: ?FinalTerms.Term = null,
};

pub const Signal = union(enum) {
    message: FinalTerms.Term, //                    (payload in RECEIVER heap)
    // e48-smp-s2: a message travelling in its own fragment heap (sender-built,
    // receiver-merged-on-drain, freed after the merge — zero-leak swept on
    // every discard path).
    message_frag: *Fragment,
    // e32-t1 fix: `kill` marks the DIRECT exit/2-with-reason-kill signal — the
    // ONE untrappable signal in BEAM (erts SIGKILL): it bypasses trap_exit,
    // terminates even a system process, and the victim's exit reason is RENAMED
    // to `killed`. A LINK-propagated exit whose reason happens to be the atom
    // `kill` is an ORDINARY signal (trappable, un-renamed) — senders on the
    // link-cascade paths leave the default false.
    // N8 (DIVERGENCE 622): exit/DOWN reasons are FRAGMENT-carried — the old
    // send-time gcCopy INTO the target's heap raced the target's own unlocked
    // appends (run-loop conses, off-L_v drain merges): interleaved arena
    // writers corrupted term handles (LAW n8-exit-during-run crashed 3/3
    // before this fix). The sender copies the reason into a fresh fragment
    // heap it owns; the receiver merges on drain (owner-side, safe) — the S2
    // message-fragment discipline applied to the exit surface.
    exit_sig: struct { rfrag: *Fragment, kill: bool = false },
    down: struct { ref: u64, rfrag: *Fragment },
    link_req,
    unlink_req,
};

const SigMsg = struct { from: Pid, stamp: u64, sig: Signal };

/// E5.5 (Task 5): one entry of a process-LOCAL alias table (erts:
/// `alias/1`/`unalias/1` + `monitor(process,Pid,[{alias,Mode}])`). An alias is a
/// reference the OWNING process mints so that a `Ref ! Msg` from any process is
/// routed to the owner's mailbox — but ONLY while the alias is `active`. The
/// alias is a DELIVERY-time signal behavior (DIVERGENCE entry 19), not a term
/// construction one: the ref TERM already exists (E3.5); this table is the
/// missing piece that consults it on send and retires it. Retirement paths:
///   - `unalias/1` — the owner explicitly deactivates it (`active := false`);
///   - the owner's DEATH — a dead proc is skipped by `resolveAliasOwner`, so
///     every alias it owns is implicitly retired (no per-entry work);
///   - a monitor 'DOWN' — for an alias minted by `monitor(process,Pid,[{alias,
///     Mode}])` where `Mode /= explicit_unalias` (`retire_on_down = true`), the
///     alias auto-retires when its monitor's 'DOWN' is delivered (erts' default).
const AliasEntry = struct { key: u64, active: bool, retire_on_down: bool = false, priority: bool = false };

/// E5.6 (Task 6): a PARKED spawn-reply — the async window of the spawn/request
/// protocol. `erts_internal:spawn_request/4` mints `reqid`, spawns+runs `child`,
/// and parks `reply` (`{spawn_reply, ReqId, ok, ChildPid}`, built in the `to`
/// caller's heap) here instead of delivering it in the same reduction. The next
/// scheduler tick (`deliverParkedSpawn`, run at `grant` start) signals it to
/// `to`; until then `spawn_request_abandon(ReqId)` can CANCEL it (→ `true`, the
/// child orphaned). Once delivered/cancelled the entry is gone, so abandon then
/// answers `false` — the deterministic-window model of the real SMP race
/// (DIVERGENCE entry 72).
/// E8.2i's `dist_spawn_request/4` no-carrier branch deliberately does NOT use
/// this parked success path: OTP's not-connected direct BIF sends a local
/// `{Tag, Ref, error, Reason}` reply immediately and returns only the request
/// shape, with no child process and no carrier state.
/// sender-narrowing N1: `vhash` is PRECOMPUTED at park time (the requester is
/// owned by the parking worker, its heap quiescent) — delivery must NOT hash
/// the requester's heap, which may belong to a proc RUNNING UNLOCKED on
/// another worker by then (the latent delivery-time read the N1 slice closes).
const ParkedSpawn = struct { reqid: u64, to: Pid, child: Pid, reply: FinalTerms.Term, vhash: u64 };

/// E8.2j/E8.2l (Task 2): a distribution connection entry. `new_connection/1`
/// creates a pending handle before any carrier/channel exists; E8.2l adds the
/// first live state transition behind that handle. The handle ref is stable for
/// the node's DistEntry; pending entries reuse the connection id until abort;
/// live entries are observable as connected peers and may record a local
/// controller owner, but still do not expose a real socket or remote process.
const DistEntry = struct {
    node: ta.AtomIdx,
    handle_ref: u64,
    conn_id: u64,
    pending: bool,
    live: bool = false,
    controller: ?FinalTerms.Term = null,
    input_handler: ?FinalTerms.Term = null,
    peer_creation: u32 = 0,
    peer_dflags: i64 = 0,
};

/// E8.2q: VM-owned node monitor registrations over live DistEntry rows. This is
/// the proc.Vm bridge for `monitor_node/2,3`; it is not a socket/controller.
const DistNodeMonitor = struct { node: ta.AtomIdx, watcher: Pid };

/// E8.2v: outbound remote-exit observations. This is the in-VM bridge from
/// `exit_signal/2,3` to a live DistEntry; it records the exact frame intent a
/// future socket/TCP carrier must serialize. Terms remain in the caller heap,
/// matching existing parked-reply observations; E8.2y adds owned ETF bytes so a
/// carrier drain never has to read that caller heap.
pub const DistRemoteExit = struct { from: Pid, node: ta.AtomIdx, to: u64, creation: u32, reason: FinalTerms.Term, reason_wire: []u8 };
const DIST_SYSTEM_PID: Pid = std.math.maxInt(Pid);

/// E6.2 (Task 2): a LIVE timer the Vm owns — one entry in the `timer_wheel`
/// (the M-era wheel algebra, now driving real receive timers + the `send_after`/
/// `start_timer` BIF family; DIVERGENCE entry 5's residual). The wheel supplies
/// the ORDERING/at-most-once/cancel-then-fire-impossible guarantees (its payload
/// is this entry's index in `Vm.timer_meta`); this struct carries the SEMANTIC
/// action a fire performs. `deadline` is an ABSOLUTE virtual-clock reading (ms).
const TimerKind = enum { receive_timeout, send_after, start_timer, apoptosis_tick };
const TimerMeta = struct {
    kind: TimerKind,
    owner: Pid, //                the process that armed it (source heap for msg/dest)
    dest: FinalTerms.Term = 0, // owner-heap; the delivery target (pid|name); unused for receive_timeout
    msg: FinalTerms.Term = 0, //  owner-heap; the message payload; unused for receive_timeout
    tref: u64 = 0, //             user-facing TRef key; 0 for a receive_timeout (no TRef)
    deadline: u64, //             absolute virtual-clock ms
    wheel_id: timer_wheel.TimerId,
    live: bool = true, //         false once fired or cancelled (at-most-once at the Vm layer too)
};

pub const Event = union(enum) {
    grant: Pid, //                       scheduler gave this pid a slice
    send: struct { from: Pid, to: Pid, stamp: u64, vhash: u64 },
    deliver: struct { from: Pid, to: Pid, stamp: u64, vhash: u64 },
    spawned: struct { parent: Pid, child: Pid },
    exited: struct { pid: Pid, rhash: u64 },
    down_delivered: struct { to: Pid, ref: u64 },
};

pub const Process = struct {
    /// gap-b-5a (SMP step 5a, the ENABLING REFACTOR): process OWNERSHIP and the
    /// deferred-wake flag were RELOCATED OUT of `Process` into the `L_c`-domain
    /// arrays `Vm.owned[pid]` / `Vm.pending_wake_a[pid]` (indexed by the integer
    /// pid). The relocation is what lets the SMP worker's coordinator section
    /// (discover→pick→mark-owned) touch NO `procs` pointer: `procs` is an
    /// `ArrayList(*Process)` whose backing array reallocates on `append`, so
    /// `procs.items[pid]` races a concurrent spawn — but a pid is a plain integer
    /// and the `owned`/`pending_wake_a` arrays are grown ONLY under `L_c` at spawn
    /// (pointer-stable, pid == index), so the coordinator marks ownership by
    /// `owned.items[pid] = id` with NO pointer deref. See `Vm.owned` /
    /// `Vm.pending_wake_a` / `Vm.ready` and `docs/OTP30_SMP_EPOCH_PLAN.md` step 5.
    // e50-tracing: erlang:trace/3 target state — a tracer pid + flag set.
    // When `tracer` is set and the matching flag is on, the signal layer
    // delivers {trace, Pid, Tag, ...} to the tracer at the event point.
    tracer: ?Pid = null,
    trace_send: bool = false,
    trace_recv: bool = false,
    machine: ia.Machine,
    prog: ia.Program,
    sigq: std.ArrayList(SigMsg), // incoming, per-pair stamped
    // S-epoch Option B: `L_sigq` — this process's OWN signal-queue leaf lock
    // (see `SigqLock`). Guards `sigq` alone; lowest rank in the total order.
    sigq_lock: SigqLock = .{},
    links: std.ArrayList(Pid),
    monitors_held: std.ArrayList(struct { ref: u64, target: Pid }),
    monitored_by: std.ArrayList(struct { ref: u64, watcher: Pid }),
    // E5.5 (Task 5): this process's LOCAL alias table (see `AliasEntry`). Empty
    // for a fresh spawn (`alias/1` mints the first entry); a send to a ref
    // consults EVERY live process's table via `resolveAliasOwner`. Ownership is
    // genuinely process-local — `unalias/1` only ever touches the CALLER's table.
    aliases: std.ArrayList(AliasEntry),
    // E4.1: the trap_exit flag lives on the Machine (`machine.trap_exit`, set by
    // `process_flag(trap_exit, _)` — bifs/procsys.process_flag_2 — and read here by
    // the exit-signal handler). Before E4.1 a SEPARATE `Process.trap_exit` shadowed
    // it, so a compiled `.beam`'s `process_flag(trap_exit, true)` set the Machine
    // field while the scheduler read the Process field — the flag never took effect
    // end-to-end (an untrapped linked exit killed the parent). Unifying on the one
    // Machine field is what makes trap_exit reachable from a compiled `.beam`.
    // gap-procinfo-initial-call (DIVERGENCE 754): the spawn MFA {M,F,A} recorded at
    // birth by `resolveSpawnMFA` — what `process_info(P, initial_call)` returns
    // ({M, F, length(Args)}). Atom indices are GLOBAL/stable, so storing indices (not
    // heap terms) is heap-independent + survives GC. `null` for a hand-built/root
    // process with no spawn MFA, which `procInfoValue` denotes as OTP's documented
    // default `{erlang, apply, 2}` (host-verified: the root proc's initial_call).
    initial_call: ?struct { m: ta.AtomIdx, f: ta.AtomIdx, a: u32 } = null,
    alive: bool = true,
    // E4.3 (Task 3): a SYSTEM process (spawned via
    // `erts_internal:spawn_system_process/3`). The kernel bring-up set runs as
    // system processes so an unrelated crash in the supervision tree cannot cascade
    // into them: a system process does NOT die from an untrapped abnormal exit
    // SIGNAL delivered over a link (the no-kill-cascade law) — only its own crash
    // or an explicit `exit(Pid, kill)` stops it. Ordinary spawns leave this false,
    // so every pre-E4.3 process/link law is unchanged.
    system: bool = false,
    // E4.6 (Task 6): a PASSIVE group-leader io fixture (the `user`/`standard_io`
    // server). It runs NO compiled code — its machine sits permanently
    // `.suspended` and the scheduler never grants it (a suspended proc is not
    // `.running`; `signal` also leaves it suspended, see the `io_gl` guard there).
    // It is serviced ONLY by `Vm.serviceGroupLeader`, which drains its mailbox of
    // `{io_request,From,ReplyAs,Request}` messages and replies. Ordinary spawns
    // leave this false, so every pre-E4.6 scheduler/receive law is unchanged.
    io_gl: bool = false,
    // E6.2 (Task 2): the index into `Vm.timer_meta` of this process's ARMED
    // receive-timeout entry (`receive after N`), or null. Set when `wait_timeout`
    // finite arms; CLEARED (and the wheel entry cancelled — exactly-once) when a
    // message wakes the process before the timeout (`Vm.signal`), and cleared when
    // the entry fires (`Vm.fireTimer`). The lost-wakeup guard's timer half.
    recv_timer: ?usize = null,
    // E6.4 (Task 4) / gap-b-6a: the process-SUSPENSION count RELOCATED off `Process`
    // into the `L_c`-domain per-pid array `Vm.suspend_a[pid]` (symmetric to gap-b-5a's
    // `owned`/`pending_wake_a` relocation). The count is `L_c`-domain because the SMP
    // epilogue's schedulable-gated requeue must read it under `L_c` ALONE (a suspend/
    // resume BIF on ANOTHER worker mutates it concurrently; keeping it a `Process`
    // field forced the requeue back under `L_v`). Semantics are UNCHANGED: still the
    // suspend/resume counting MONOID, still ORTHOGONAL to `machine.status`, still
    // gating `schedulable`. See `Vm.suspend_a` + `schedulable` below.
    // gap-reduction-cost-model R4 ({R} LIVE scheduling-latency): the value of the
    // global `Vm.grant_reduction_total` AT THE END of this process's PREVIOUS grant
    // (i.e. after its own reductions were folded in). On this process's NEXT grant,
    // `grant_reduction_total - last_grant_total` is exactly the reductions consumed
    // by OTHER processes in the interim — its per-grant scheduling latency. Only
    // read/written when `Vm.latency_histogram != null` (opt-in; 0 otherwise, so a
    // histogram-free run is byte-identical to before this seam).
    last_grant_total: u64 = 0,
};

/// E6.4 (Task 4) / gap-b-6a: the scheduler's schedulability predicate — a process is
/// granted CPU iff it is alive, its machine is `.running`, AND it is not BIF-suspended
/// (`Vm.suspend_a[pid] == 0`). The single point every run queue / discovery / migration
/// / quiescence site consults, so suspension gates every scheduler engine (the
/// sequential round-robin ORACLE and the SMP model + thread engines) uniformly.
/// gap-b-6a: takes `(vm, pid)` (was `*const Process`) so the suspend gate reads the
/// `L_c`-domain `suspend_a` — `alive`/`status` stay `Process` (owner-exclusive while
/// owned), so at the SMP epilogue this whole predicate is safe under `L_c` ALONE.
pub fn schedulable(vm: *const Vm, pid: Pid) bool {
    const p = vm.procs.items[pid];
    return p.alive and p.machine.status == .running and vm.suspend_a.items[pid].load(.monotonic) == 0;
}

/// E5.4: a live os-port the Vm owns at runtime — a Port NUMBER (the port term's
/// `number` field), the owning (connected) process, and the real child behind the
/// Stratum-C `os_port` seam. `data` holds `port_set_data`'s opaque term, stored on
/// the OWNER process's heap (copied in on set, out on get). This is the runtime
/// ENGINE half of the E4.5/E5.4 port ENDPOINT algebra (Stratum B): its behavior is
/// admitted by the differential erl-corpus (both VMs drive the SAME OS program).
const RunPort = struct {
    number: u32,
    connected: Pid,
    live: os_port.LivePort,
    alive: bool,
    data: ?FinalTerms.Term = null, // stored on the `connected` proc's heap
    // E6.7 (Task 7): the port NAME — the command string as given to
    // `open_port({spawn, Cmd}, _)` — reported by `port_info(P, name)`/`ports/0`'s
    // proplist as a charlist. `gpa`-owned, freed in `Vm.deinit`.
    name: []u8 = &.{},
    // gap-open-port-dispatch (DIVERGENCE 594): the port DATA MODE. OTP defaults a
    // port to `list` — `{Port,{data,String}}` delivers a CHARLIST; the `binary`
    // open-option switches it to `{Port,{data,Binary}}`. Was hard-wired to binary
    // (a real divergence the cat round-trip surfaced: zigvm gave <<...>> where OTP
    // gives "ping\n"). Default false = list mode, EQ to OTP's default.
    binary_mode: bool = false,
    // gap-open-port-exit-status (Slice B): the `exit_status` open-option — when the
    // child exits on its OWN, deliver `{Port,{exit_status,Code}}` to the owner. NOT
    // delivered on an explicit port_close (OTP: `no_es_after_close`).
    exit_status: bool = false,
    // gap-open-port-line (Slice C): the `{line, N}` open-option — 0 == not line-mode;
    // else the max line length. Line mode delivers `{Port,{data,{eol|noeol,Line}}}`
    // (port_algebra.lineFrames) instead of one `{Port,{data,Out}}`.
    line_width: usize = 0,
};

// gap-socket-real-tcp: a live gen_tcp/gen_udp socket held across the trap
// boundary. A socket term is `{'$socket', number}`; `socketOf` maps it back to a
// RunSocket. `kind` distinguishes a listener (accept target) from a connected
// stream / a udp endpoint. `port` is the bound port (listeners/udp) so a peer can
// connect/sendto. Every fd is closed in `close`/`deinit` (idempotent) so no fd
// outlives the Vm — zero leaks over the whole table.
const RunSocket = struct {
    number: u32,
    fd: i32,
    kind: enum { listen, stream, udp },
    port: u16 = 0,
    alive: bool,
    // gap-socket-active-mode: {active,X} + the owner process to deliver
    // {tcp,S,Data} to. 0=passive, 1=once, 2=true. The SCHEDULER (not setopts)
    // polls active stream sockets and delivers — never a synchronous poll.
    active: u8 = 0,
    owner: Pid = 0,
    // gap-cowboy-trap-accept + gap-accept-waiters-queue (DIVERGENCE 647): the FIFO
    // QUEUE of processes SUSPENDED in gen_tcp:accept(_, infinity) on this LISTEN
    // socket. A ranch acceptor POOL (default 10) all block here AT ONCE, so a single
    // slot is wrong — it silently overwrote to ONE registered waiter (9 lost, a
    // latent robustness gap under concurrent accepts). `pollAcceptSockets` accept4()s
    // each pending connection non-blocking and RESUMES the FRONT (oldest) waiter with
    // {ok, ConnSock}; a served waiter re-arms via its next accept (re-enqueue). So an
    // infinity-accept never blocks the drive AND the whole pool is served fairly.
    accept_waiters: std.ArrayList(Pid) = .empty,
};

// gap-file-handle-io: a live file:open/2 handle held across the trap boundary.
// An IoDevice term is `{file_descriptor, prim_file, number}`; `fdOf` maps it back
// to a RunFd. The real fd lives in `prim_file.Handle`. Every handle is closed in
// `doFileClose`/`deinit` (idempotent — `alive` guards a double-close) so no fd
// outlives the Vm — the zero-leak / fd-exhaustion guarantee (FMEA FM-FD-1).
const RunFd = struct {
    number: u32,
    handle: prim_file.Handle,
    alive: bool,
    // gap-file-seq-io: the SEQUENTIAL fd offset. pread/pwrite (file:pread/pwrite) are
    // positioned+stateless; file:read/2 / write/2 read/write AT this offset then
    // ADVANCE it, and file:position/2 seeks it. Independent of the OS fd offset (we
    // always pread/pwrite at an explicit position) so the two APIs never interfere.
    pos: u64 = 0,
};

/// `L_c` — the COHERENCY-DOMAIN lock (`gap-b-4`, the CRUX of the SMP coarse→per-
/// lock transition). A self-contained atomic spinlock, IDENTICAL in shape to
/// `SmpEngine.VmLock` (`L_v`), `timer_wheel.TimerLock` (`L_timer`), and
/// `registry.RegLock` (`L_reg`): acquire `.acquire`, release `.release`, a full
/// happens-before fence around every coherency-domain mutation/observation.
///
/// THE COHERENCY DOMAIN it protects — as ONE unit: the GLOBAL parked lists
/// (`Vm.parked_spawn`/`parked_systask`), the per-scheduler RUN QUEUES
/// (`SmpEngine.ThreadCtx.qs`), the PUSH-BASED ready inbox + dedup bit
/// (`Vm.ready`/`Vm.queued`, gap-b-5a), process OWNERSHIP + the deferred-wake flag
/// (`Vm.owned[pid]`/`Vm.pending_wake_a[pid]` — RELOCATED off `Process` by gap-b-5a
/// so the coordinator marks ownership by pid-INDEX, no `procs` pointer deref), and
/// the OWNED COUNT (`ThreadCtx.owned_count`). These are one coherency unit — a
/// grant's drain-ready + pick-from-queue + mark-owned, or release's clear-owned +
/// requeue, or the parked publish/drain, are each ATOMIC under `L_c` (conservation:
/// a pid is runnable on ≤1 scheduler — never observably in two queues nor lost
/// between queue and ownership).
///
/// LOCK-ORDER (the safety-critical invariant `L_v → L_c → {L_reg,L_timer} → L_e`,
/// UCA-SMP-9 / C-8): `L_c` sits STRICTLY BELOW `L_v` and STRICTLY ABOVE
/// `{L_reg,L_timer}` and `L_e`. The acyclicity of the WHOLE order rests on:
///
///   (1) NO BACK-EDGE `L_c → L_v` — BY CONSTRUCTION. Every `L_c` critical
///       section lives in a coherency-domain method/site that references only
///       the domain (parked lists, `qs`, `ready`/`queued`, `owned`/`owned_count`),
///       never a `VmLock`/`ThreadCtx.mutex`, so it CANNOT acquire `L_v`. Every live
///       `L_c` acquisition happens while the caller EITHER already holds `L_v`
///       (the epilogue + `interpret` + the grant-event OOM rollback — the
///       sanctioned DOWN-edge `L_v → L_c`) OR holds NO lock at all: the SMP
///       worker's coordinator section (`gap-b-5`, `drainReady → pick → mark-owned`)
///       and the single-thread `driveModel`/`grant`. The gap-b-5 flip made the
///       coordinator take `L_c` WITHOUT `L_v` — that is SOUND for acyclicity: the
///       order forbids acquiring a HIGHER lock while holding a LOWER one, and an
///       `L_c`-without-`L_v` section NEVER reaches UP to `L_v` while holding `L_c`
///       (on every latch/error path it RELEASES `L_c` first, then takes `L_v`). So
///       the observed order is always `L_v → L_c` (down) or disjoint `L_c` / `L_v`
///       sections, never `L_c → L_v` (up).
///   (2) THE SANCTIONED DOWN-EDGE `L_c → L_timer` IS REAL, IN-ORDER, and the
///       only cross-lock nesting under `L_c`: `deliverParkedSpawn`/
///       `deliverParkedSysTask` hold `L_c` and drive `signal → applyWake →
///       timers.cancel`, which acquires `L_timer`. Because `L_timer` is BELOW
///       `L_c`, this is the sanctioned direction — no cycle. `L_c` is never
///       re-acquired on that chain (the spinlock is non-reentrant; `signal`/
///       `applyWake` touch no coherency-domain lock), so no self-deadlock.
///   (3) NON-NESTING among `L_c` sites — no `L_c`-holding site calls another
///       `L_c`-taking site (`grant`→deliverParkedSpawn→deliverParkedSysTask→
///       grantOnly→applyStagedParks is a SEQUENCE of disjoint `L_c` sections,
///       never nested), so `L_c` never self-deadlocks.
///
/// BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVING through gap-b-5: `denote` is UNCHANGED. gap-b-4 nested
/// `L_c` inside `L_v` (inert); gap-b-5 RELEASED `L_v` around the coordinator so
/// `L_c` alone guards the drain/pick/mark-owned — now a worker in its `L_c`-alone
/// coordinator no longer blocks a peer that only needs `L_v` (its own
/// `deliverParked*`/`drainSignals`/grant-event), and vice-versa (PARTIAL overlap;
/// two coordinators still serialize on `L_c`, and the trap remains `L_v`-global —
/// full overlap awaits gap-b-6a). gap-b-5b (LANDED) gave `vm.events` its own
/// LOWEST leaf lock `L_events` (see `EventLock`/`emitEvent`), so the event
/// appends that today ride inside `L_v` holds are individually linearizable — a
/// prerequisite for later shrinking those `L_v` holds. The full suite + the
/// E6.1 scheduler-count-invariance/work-stealing-conservation laws + the gap-b-5
/// re-witness + `--verify-formal` are the denote-invariance witness.
/// See `docs/OTP30_SMP_EPOCH_PLAN.md` steps 4–5 + `DIVERGENCE_LOG` gap-b.
pub const CoherencyLock = struct {
    held: std.atomic.Value(bool) = std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false),
    pub fn lock(self: *CoherencyLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquire(.l_c);
        while (self.held.swap(true, .acquire)) std.Thread.yield() catch {};
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquired(.l_c);
    }
    pub fn unlock(self: *CoherencyLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onRelease(.l_c);
        self.held.store(false, .release);
    }
};

/// `L_events` — the global event log's own lock (`gap-b-5b`, the LOWEST leaf of
/// the SMP coarse→per-lock transition). A self-contained atomic spinlock,
/// IDENTICAL in shape to `SmpEngine.VmLock` (`L_v`), `CoherencyLock` (`L_c`),
/// `timer_wheel.TimerLock` (`L_timer`), and `registry.RegLock` (`L_reg`):
/// acquire `.acquire`, release `.release`, a full happens-before fence around
/// every mutation of `Vm.events`.
///
/// THE DOMAIN it protects: the ONE global event log `Vm.events` (the
/// grant/send/deliver/spawned/exited/down_delivered trace ring). Every APPEND
/// flows through `Vm.emitEvent`, which is the ONLY writer and holds `L_events`
/// for exactly the append. Reads of `Vm.events.items` happen post-join
/// (single-threaded, after all scheduler threads have joined) so they observe a
/// stable log with no concurrent writer.
///
/// LOCK-ORDER (the safety-critical invariant
/// `L_v → L_c → {L_reg,L_timer} → L_e → L_events`, UCA-SMP-9 / C-8): `L_events`
/// sits STRICTLY BELOW every other lock — it is the LOWEST leaf. `emitEvent`'s
/// body references NOTHING but `self.l_events`, `self.events`, `self.gpa`, and
/// the caller's event value — it holds NO reference to `ThreadCtx.mutex`
/// (`L_v`), `l_c` (`L_c`), the registry, the timer wheel, or the ETS lock, so it
/// CANNOT acquire ANY higher lock. Therefore NO path acquires a higher lock
/// while holding `L_events`: the back-edge is impossible BY CONSTRUCTION (proof
/// by type — every outer lock is unreachable from `emitEvent`'s scope). Every
/// live acquisition happens while the caller EITHER already holds a higher lock
/// (the SMP grant epilogue / `interpret` under `L_v`, the sanctioned DOWN-edge
/// `L_v → L_events`) OR holds no lock at all (single-thread `driveModel`/
/// `grant`); either way the observed order is `… → L_events`, never the reverse.
/// See `docs/OTP30_SMP_EPOCH_PLAN.md` step 5b + `DIVERGENCE_LOG` gap-b.
pub const EventLock = struct {
    held: std.atomic.Value(bool) = std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false),
    pub fn lock(self: *EventLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquire(.l_events);
        while (self.held.swap(true, .acquire)) std.Thread.yield() catch {};
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquired(.l_events);
    }
    pub fn unlock(self: *EventLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onRelease(.l_events);
        self.held.store(false, .release);
    }
};

/// `L_v` — the Stratum-C VM lock (S-epoch Option B: LIFTED from
/// `SmpEngine.ThreadCtx` to module scope so `drainSignals` can escalate onto it
/// via `tl_drain_lv` — a `Vm` method cannot name a type private to the engine).
/// A self-contained atomic spinlock. Acquire is `.acquire`, release is
/// `.release` — a full happens-before fence around every VM mutation, so all
/// shared VM state (procs, next_stamp, heaps) is touched by exactly one thread
/// at a time. The TOP of the total order `L_v→L_c→{L_reg,L_timer}→L_e→L_events→L_sigq`.
pub const VmLock = struct {
    held: std.atomic.Value(bool) = std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false),
    pub fn lock(self: *VmLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquire(.l_v);
        while (self.held.swap(true, .acquire)) std.Thread.yield() catch {};
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquired(.l_v);
    }
    pub fn unlock(self: *VmLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onRelease(.l_v);
        self.held.store(false, .release);
    }
};

/// `L_sigq` — the per-`Process` SIGNAL-QUEUE leaf lock (S-epoch Option B, the
/// sigq de-globalization). A self-contained atomic spinlock, identical in shape
/// to `VmLock`/`CoherencyLock`/`EventLock`. It guards EXACTLY one process's
/// `sigq` vector: the sender-side `append` (`signal`/`signalTrace`), the
/// receiver-side batch DETACH in `drainSignals`, the demonitor-flush sweep, and
/// the hibernate emptiness read. The LOWEST leaf of the total order (rank 5,
/// below `L_events`): always acquired last, and NOTHING is acquired while
/// holding it — every critical section is a pure vector op with no lock reach,
/// so a back-edge is impossible by construction (UCA-SMP-9).
pub const SigqLock = struct {
    held: std.atomic.Value(bool) = std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false),
    pub fn lock(self: *SigqLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquire(.l_sigq);
        while (self.held.swap(true, .acquire)) std.Thread.yield() catch {};
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquired(.l_sigq);
    }
    pub fn unlock(self: *SigqLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onRelease(.l_sigq);
        self.held.store(false, .release);
    }
};

/// S-epoch Option B: the per-WORKER `L_v` ESCALATION pointer. `drainSignals`
/// has ~80 callers, almost all of which run while `L_v` is ALREADY held (the
/// interpret-path drains) or single-threaded (`driveModel`) — for them this is
/// `null` and behavior is byte-identical. ONLY `SmpEngine.worker` SECTION 2
/// sets it (around its epilogue drain, which now runs OFF `L_v`): the drain
/// then briefly takes `L_v` for the `procs` deref + sigq detach, releases it
/// across the owner-exclusive message-delivery bulk, and RE-escalates under it
/// for the arms that reach `L_v`-domain state (`.exit_sig`→terminate,
/// `.down`/`.link_req`/`.unlink_req`→links/monitors/aliases — read under `L_v`
/// by `resolveAliasOwner`/`process_info` — and the emitTrace→signal sub-path's
/// `procs` deref). threadlocal ⇒ no shared-state race; it MUST stay `null` for
/// any drain that runs under a held `L_v` or the non-reentrant spinlock
/// self-deadlocks (the gotcha the S-epoch handover documents).
/// N4 (the sigq-detach merge, DIVERGENCE 618): a STABLE, lock-free-readable
/// process-box index mirroring `procs` (same boxes, same pid order). The
/// `procs` ArrayList reallocs on spawn, so any deref needs `L_v` — this was
/// the relocated drain's ONLY remaining `L_v` need. Fixed-root two-level
/// table: segment pointers never move once created; the WRITER (spawn, always
/// under the caller's `L_v` or single-threaded boot) writes the slot then
/// release-publishes `count`; a READER acquire-loads `count` and indexes —
/// no lock, no realloc, no torn pointer (the slot + segment-pointer writes
/// happen-before the count publish). MIRROR LAW: count == procs.len and every
/// index holds the same box (`n4-stable-mirror`).
pub const StableProcIndex = struct {
    const SEG_SHIFT = 10;
    const SEG_SIZE = 1 << SEG_SHIFT; // 1024 boxes per segment
    const MAX_SEGS = 1024; // ~1M live procs; append fails closed beyond
    segs: [MAX_SEGS]?*[SEG_SIZE]*Process = [_]?*[SEG_SIZE]*Process{null} ** MAX_SEGS,
    count: std.atomic.Value(usize) = std.atomic.Value(usize).init(0),

    fn append(self: *StableProcIndex, gpa: std.mem.Allocator, p: *Process) !void {
        const i = self.count.load(.monotonic);
        const si = i >> SEG_SHIFT;
        if (si >= MAX_SEGS) return error.OutOfMemory;
        if (self.segs[si] == null) self.segs[si] = try gpa.create([SEG_SIZE]*Process);
        self.segs[si].?[i & (SEG_SIZE - 1)] = p;
        self.count.store(i + 1, .release); // publish AFTER the slot write
    }
    fn get(self: *const StableProcIndex, i: usize) ?*Process {
        if (i >= self.count.load(.acquire)) return null;
        return self.segs[i >> SEG_SHIFT].?[i & (SEG_SIZE - 1)];
    }
    fn deinit(self: *StableProcIndex, gpa: std.mem.Allocator) void {
        for (self.segs) |seg| if (seg) |sp| gpa.destroy(sp);
    }
};

/// N7 (L_events sharding, DIVERGENCE 621): one seq-stamped event destined for
/// the per-worker shard buffer. `seq` is minted by the LOCK-FREE atomic
/// `Vm.event_seq` at emit time — the linearization point that replaces the
/// `L_events` lock's append order. Shards are per-worker (threadlocal
/// registration), each internally seq-ascending; the join-time merge sorts by
/// `seq`, reproducing a valid single-log linearization.
const SeqEvent = struct { seq: u64, ev: Event };

/// N9-Q1: the atomic-ownership sentinel (no worker).
pub const NO_OWNER: u32 = 0xFFFF_FFFF;

/// N9-Q1: `L_q` — one per-worker run-queue lock (rank between `L_c` and
/// `{L_reg,L_timer}`). Identical spinlock shape to the other leaves. NEVER
/// more than one held at a time: the steal pops from the victim's queue and
/// owns the pid IN HAND (no second QLock), and the quiescence verdict is
/// atomics-only (no sweep).
pub const QLock = struct {
    held: std.atomic.Value(bool) = std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false),
    pub fn lock(self: *QLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquire(.l_q);
        while (self.held.swap(true, .acquire)) std.Thread.yield() catch {};
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onAcquired(.l_q);
    }
    pub fn unlock(self: *QLock) void {
        if (comptime smp_trace.enabled) smp_trace.onRelease(.l_q);
        self.held.store(false, .release);
    }
};

/// N7: the per-WORKER event shard registration. Set ONLY by `SmpEngine.worker`
/// for its own thread; every other emitter (driveModel/grant/boot — single-
/// threaded or pre/post-run) sees `null` and takes the classic `L_events`
/// global-append path unchanged.
threadlocal var tl_event_buf: ?*std.ArrayList(SeqEvent) = null;

/// N7: merge per-worker event shards into `vm.events`, ordered by `seq` — the
/// exact linearization the `L_events` lock's append order used to produce
/// (each emit's linearization point is its `event_seq` fetchAdd). Total: every
/// shard entry lands exactly once; the shards are left empty. Single-threaded
/// (post-join). LAW n7-event-shard-merge pins order + conservation; the
/// threaded suite (per-pair order, deliver-count, vhash agreement) re-proves
/// the quotient end-to-end.
fn mergeEventShards(vm: *Vm, shards: []std.ArrayList(SeqEvent)) !void {
    var total: usize = 0;
    for (shards) |sh| total += sh.items.len;
    if (total == 0) return;
    var all: std.ArrayList(SeqEvent) = .empty;
    defer all.deinit(vm.gpa);
    try all.ensureTotalCapacity(vm.gpa, total);
    for (shards) |sh| all.appendSliceAssumeCapacity(sh.items);
    std.mem.sort(SeqEvent, all.items, {}, struct {
        fn lt(_: void, a: SeqEvent, b: SeqEvent) bool {
            return a.seq < b.seq;
        }
    }.lt);
    try vm.events.ensureUnusedCapacity(vm.gpa, total);
    for (all.items) |se| vm.events.appendAssumeCapacity(se.ev);
    for (shards) |*sh| sh.clearRetainingCapacity();
}

threadlocal var tl_drain_lv: ?*VmLock = null;
/// Option B acquire-merge (measured: the A/B probe attributed a 4× `L_v`
/// acquire-count rise, partly the worker's separate pre-deref hold): the
/// relocated drain already derefs the stable `Process` box under its detach
/// `L_v` hold — it stashes the box here so SECTION 2 reads it WITHOUT a second
/// acquire. Same thread, written under `L_v`, consumed immediately after the
/// drain returns; `null` when the drain found no live proc.
threadlocal var tl_drain_vp: ?*Process = null;

pub const Vm = struct {
    gpa: std.mem.Allocator,
    atoms: *AtomTable,
    procs: std.ArrayList(*Process),
    // N4: the stable mirror of `procs` (see `StableProcIndex`).
    stable_procs: StableProcIndex = .{},
    events: std.ArrayList(Event),
    // N7: the lock-free event linearization counter (see `SeqEvent`).
    event_seq: std.atomic.Value(u64) = std.atomic.Value(u64).init(0),
    // gap-fs-autoloader: the OPTIONAL filesystem autoload seam. `null` (the
    // default) = no autoloading anywhere — every spawn gets
    // `autoload_enabled=false` and dispatch is byte-identical to before this
    // seam existed. Set (by `cli.runMulti` from RunOptions, or a law directly),
    // a `dispatchMFA` miss traps `ensure_loaded` and the Vm searches
    // `code_path` (dir subpaths under `root`, in order) for `Module.beam`,
    // reads it BOUNDED via prim_file (the proven fd seam), and commits it via
    // `code.loadBeamBytes` into the CALLING process's runtime code table.
    autoload: ?AutoloadCfg = null,
    /// e49-wire-real-file-io: the OPTIONAL real-file seam — file:read_file/1
    /// and file:write_file/2 route to the PROVEN prim_file fd seam (E4.4) under
    /// this `root`. `null` (default) = no filesystem; file BIFs report
    /// {error,enoent}-shaped closed-world results (byte-identical to before).
    fs: ?FsCfg = null,
    // gap-erl-cli-args (DIVERGENCE 598): the OPTIONAL init argument view — the parsed
    // `erl`/`init` argv (flags + `-extra` plain args) so `init:get_plain_arguments/0`
    // and `init:get_argument/1` answer from a compiled beam. `null` (default) = no CLI
    // args (get_plain_arguments → [], get_argument → error). Slices BORROW argv bytes
    // (alive for the whole run — the init_arena in zigvm_main).
    init_args: ?InitArgsView = null,
    // gap-reduction-cost-model R4 ({R} LIVE scheduling-latency instrumentation):
    // the OPT-IN histogram the drive/grant path folds each grant's scheduling
    // latency into. `null` (the default) = NO instrumentation anywhere — the
    // grant path is byte-identical to before this seam (zero overhead when
    // absent). When set (by a law or `--latency-oracle` via the bench CLI), each
    // `grantOnly` records `grant_reduction_total - proc.last_grant_total` (the
    // reductions OTHER processes burned since this proc's previous grant) and then
    // advances both counters. See `LAW e50-t4 LIVE-LATENCY`.
    latency_histogram: ?*LatencyHistogram = null,
    // gap-r2c-behavioral-parity (DIVERGENCE 607, R2c epoch Inc1): the OPT-IN
    // reduction-preemption switch. `false` (default) = the R2b instr-preempt grant
    // (via runProduction/the JIT), byte-identical to before. `true` = grantOnly
    // preempts on the OTP-faithful per-call `m.reductions` (fuel is a REDUCTION
    // budget) via the interpreter `ia.runReductionPreempt`, with an instr fail-safe
    // (`fuel * REDUCTION_PREEMPT_INSTR_CAP_MULT`) that bounds a 0-reduction hog
    // (UCA-R2C-1). Inc1 uses the interpreter (per-op reduction granularity); the
    // native-block reduction bounding is Inc2. NOT flipped on by default anywhere.
    reduction_preempt: bool = false,
    // gap-r2c-behavioral-parity-native (DIVERGENCE 608, R2c epoch Inc2): when BOTH
    // this AND `reduction_preempt` are set, the reduction-preempted grant drives the
    // production JIT (`dispatch.runProductionReds`) instead of the interpreter
    // (`ia.runReductionPreempt`) — so a reduction-preempted process KEEPS its native
    // machine-code hot path. Exact by the {0,1}-reductionCost theorem (see
    // `dispatch.runProductionReds`), same yield point as the interpreter path. A
    // no-op unless `reduction_preempt` is also true; default false = Inc1 unchanged.
    reduction_preempt_native: bool = false,
    // The running total of reductions consumed across ALL grants (the global
    // grant clock the latency measure is differenced against). Only advanced when
    // `latency_histogram != null`, so it stays 0 in a histogram-free run.
    grant_reduction_total: u64 = 0,
    next_ref: u64 = 1,
    // e48-smp-s2: the stamping authority is an ATOMIC counter — under the
    // coarse lock the fetchAdd sequence is identical to the old += 1 (the M7
    // per-pair law survives unchanged); when S3 narrows the lock, stamping
    // stays a single global order without any lock at all.
    next_stamp: std.atomic.Value(u64) = std.atomic.Value(u64).init(0),
    // E5.4 (Task 4): the live os-port table + the port-number counter. A port term
    // carries `number`; `portOf` maps it back to a RunPort. Children are reaped in
    // `deinit` (idempotent) so no OS process or fd outlives the Vm.
    ports: std.ArrayList(RunPort) = .empty,
    next_port: u32 = 1,
    // gap-socket-real-tcp: the live gen_tcp/gen_udp socket table + handle counter.
    // A socket term carries `number`; `socketOf` maps it back. All fds closed in
    // `deinit` (idempotent) so no OS socket outlives the Vm.
    sockets: std.ArrayList(RunSocket) = .empty,
    // gap-file-handle-io: the live file:open/2 handle table + monotone counter.
    // An IoDevice term carries `number`; `fdOf` maps it back. All handles closed in
    // `deinit` (idempotent) so no OS fd outlives the Vm (FMEA FM-FD-1: fd-leak).
    fds: std.ArrayList(RunFd) = .empty,
    fd_counter: u32 = 0,
    next_socket: u32 = 1,
    // E5.6 (Task 6): parked spawn-request replies awaiting the next scheduler tick
    // (see `ParkedSpawn`). Empty except in the window between a `spawn_request/4`
    // and its reply's delivery/cancellation. Cleared in `deliverParkedSpawn`; the
    // reply terms live in their `to` proc's heap (freed with that proc), so
    // `deinit` only frees the list itself.
    parked_spawn: std.ArrayList(ParkedSpawn) = .empty,
    // E7.5 (Task 5): parked async system-task replies (`ParkedSysTask`), awaiting
    // the next scheduler tick (the `parked_spawn` sibling). Empty except in the
    // window between an `erts_internal:request_system_task/3,4` and its reply's
    // delivery. Cleared in `deliverParkedSysTask`; reply terms live in their `to`
    // proc's heap (freed with that proc), so this list owns no term memory.
    parked_systask: std.ArrayList(ParkedSysTask) = .empty,
    // N2a: LOCK-FREE "parked work exists" flag — armed by every publish (under
    // `L_c`), claimed by the worker top-of-loop BEFORE it takes `L_v` for the
    // parked tick. false ⇒ the idle iteration acquires NO lock. A stale-true
    // (post-abandon) costs one wasted acquire, never a stranded park.
    parked_nonempty: std.atomic.Value(bool) = std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false),
    // N5 (L_c coordinator narrowing, DIVERGENCE 619): a LOCK-FREE HINT of how
    // many pids hold the `queued` bit (ready inbox ∪ run queues). Maintained
    // at the queued-bit writes (all inside `L_c` sections or single-threaded
    // boot). A HINT only: the worker's idle peek reads it to SKIP the `L_c`
    // coordinator tick; every decision (drain/pick/quiescence) still happens
    // under `L_c` on the real state — a stale hint costs one wasted or one
    // deferred tick, never a wrong verdict.
    runnable_total: std.atomic.Value(usize) = std.atomic.Value(usize).init(0),
    // N3 (epilogue SECTION-A narrowing): LOCK-FREE "staged work exists" flag —
    // armed by the staging sites (doSpawnRequest/doRequestSystemTask, under the
    // caller's L_v), consulted by the worker epilogue so the per-grant L_v
    // acquire happens ONLY when an exit or a staged park actually needs it.
    // Cleared inside the L_v snapshot section (staging is L_v-serialized, so
    // arm/clear are totally ordered — a post-snapshot staging re-arms).
    staged_nonempty: std.atomic.Value(bool) = std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false),
    // gap-b-3 (SMP step 3): the PER-GRANT STAGING buffers for parked-list writes.
    // The trap path (`doDistSpawnRequest`/`doRequestSystemTask`) used to write the
    // GLOBAL `parked_spawn`/`parked_systask` INLINE from inside the `L_v`-held
    // `interpret` — the write that would force `L_c`-while-holding-`L_v` and close
    // the `L_v↔L_c` deadlock cycle (gap-b-4). Instead the park is STAGED here during
    // the grant and APPLIED to the global list at ONE point in the locked epilogue
    // (`applyStagedParks`, the future `L_c` site). INVARIANT: empty outside a grant's
    // run region — every grant flushes at its end (staging ⊎ global = the inline
    // global set; abandon consults BOTH so the union is identical). Owns no term
    // memory (reply terms live in their `to` proc's heap), so `deinit` frees only the
    // lists. FMEA: an OOM in the flush is all-or-nothing per list (appendSlice
    // reserves first) → never a partial/double apply, the error aborts the grant.
    staged_spawn: std.ArrayList(ParkedSpawn) = .empty,
    staged_systask: std.ArrayList(ParkedSysTask) = .empty,
    // gap-b-5a→N1: `deliverParked*` snapshot the parked list UNDER `L_c` into a
    // WORKER-LOCAL buffer (sender-narrowing N1 relocated the old Vm-shared
    // `drain_scratch_*` fields into the delivering call — they were serialized
    // only by the callers' `L_v`, an implicit coupling the top-of-loop
    // narrowing removes), clear the global list, RELEASE `L_c`, then `signal`
    // each entry with no `L_c` held (each `signal` takes `L_c` fresh — holding
    // `L_c` across the loop would re-enter the non-reentrant `L_c`).
    // gap-b-4 (SMP step 4, THE CRUX): `L_c` — the COHERENCY-DOMAIN lock. Guards
    // the parked lists + the SMP run queues + ownership + owned_count as ONE
    // coherency unit, STRICTLY below `L_v` and above `{L_reg,L_timer}` in the
    // total order `L_v→L_c→{L_reg,L_timer}→L_e` (see `CoherencyLock`). Acquired
    // ONLY while `L_v` is held (SMP worker) or lock-free (single-thread `grant`/
    // `driveModel`) — never acquires `L_v`, so the `L_v↔L_c` back-edge is
    // impossible by construction. Behaviour-preserving at this step (nested under
    // the still-held `L_v`); `gap-b-5`/`-6` narrow `L_v` to make it pay off.
    l_c: CoherencyLock = .{},
    // gap-b-5b (SMP step 5b, the LOWEST leaf): `L_events` — the global event-log
    // lock. Guards EVERY mutation of `Vm.events` (grant/send/deliver/spawned/
    // exited/down_delivered), all routed through `Vm.emitEvent`. STRICTLY BELOW
    // every other lock in the total order `L_v→L_c→{L_reg,L_timer}→L_e→L_events`
    // (see `EventLock`); acquired ONLY inside `emitEvent`, which holds no
    // reference to any higher lock, so the `L_events→(higher)` back-edge is
    // impossible by construction. Behaviour-preserving: `emitEvent` appends in
    // the SAME order the callers always did (they are serialized by the higher
    // lock they already hold, or run single-threaded), so `denote` (event order,
    // trace delivery) is UNCHANGED. `L_events` makes the append itself
    // linearizable — a prerequisite for later narrowing the `L_v` holds that
    // today wrap event writes only incidentally.
    l_events: EventLock = .{},
    // gap-b-5a (SMP step 5a, the ENABLING REFACTOR for gap-b-5): the `L_c`-domain
    // ownership state, RELOCATED out of `Process` and indexed by the integer pid.
    //   `owned[pid]`         = the worker id currently granting pid, or null (was
    //                          `Process.owned_by`). A `.n_sched` value ⇒ owned.
    //   `pending_wake_a[pid]` = a signal landed while pid was OWNED; the owner
    //                          applies the wake at release (was `Process.pending_wake`).
    // Grown ONLY in `spawn` under `L_c` (pid == index, so the arrays stay in
    // lockstep with `procs`). The point: the coordinator marks/reads ownership by
    // pid-INDEX into a pointer-STABLE array, never dereferencing the reallocating
    // `procs.items` pointer array — which is what unblocks the `L_v` narrowing
    // (gap-b-5). Both are coherency-domain state (`L_c`), like `owned_count`.
    // N9-Q1 (DIVERGENCE 623): ownership is ATOMIC (NO_OWNER sentinel) — the
    // pick takes it via fail-closed cmpxchg (SC-N9-2: a conservation breach
    // PANICS at the acquire); the Dekker wake/release protocol reads/clears it
    // with .seq_cst (SC-N9-1). No lock guards it any more.
    owned: std.ArrayList(std.atomic.Value(u32)) = .empty,
    // N9-Q1: the Dekker flag — set(.seq_cst) by the waker BEFORE it re-reads
    // `owned`; swap(false,.seq_cst)-consumed by exactly one side (the winner
    // applies the wake). acq_rel is INSUFFICIENT here (store->load visibility);
    // the Quint model specs/lc_wake_release.qnt checks precisely this.
    pending_wake_a: std.ArrayList(std.atomic.Value(bool)) = .empty,
    // gap-b-6a: the BIF-suspension count RELOCATED off `Process` (was
    // `Process.suspend_count`) into this `L_c`-domain per-pid array (pid == index),
    // grown in `spawn` in lockstep with `owned`/`pending_wake_a`/`queued`. `L_c`-domain
    // because `suspend_process`/`resume_process` mutate it under `L_c` and the SMP
    // epilogue's schedulable-gated requeue reads it under `L_c` ALONE — making the
    // whole `schedulable` predicate lockable off `L_v`, which lifts epilogue SECTION B
    // to `L_c`-alone (symmetric to gap-b-5a). The counting MONOID; `schedulable(vm,pid)`
    // gates CPU on `suspend_a[pid] == 0`.
    // N9-Q1: atomic so `schedulable` reads it lock-free from the release path
    // (mutations stay in the suspend/resume BIF sites' L_c sections).
    suspend_a: std.ArrayList(std.atomic.Value(u32)) = .empty,
    // gap-b-5a: PUSH-BASED discovery. The `ready` inbox is the `L_c`-domain FIFO
    // of pids that BECAME runnable (spawn, receive-wake, resume, receive-timeout)
    // — pushed at the wake site (via `markSchedulable`/`enqueueReadyLocked`)
    // instead of being FOUND by the worker scanning `vm.procs.items` every
    // iteration. The worker's coordinator drains `ready` into the run queues
    // (`drainReady`), so it no longer scans `procs` (no realloc race) — the second
    // half of what unblocks the `L_v` narrowing. `queued[pid]` is the exactly-once
    // dedup bit (pid is in `ready` OR some run queue): the conservation guard at
    // the SOURCE, replacing `discover`'s O(P·K) `queueCountOf==0` scan. Grown in
    // `spawn` under `L_c` alongside `owned`. Owns no term memory; freed in `deinit`.
    ready: std.ArrayList(Pid) = .empty,
    // N9-Q1: the queue-membership dedup bit is an ATOMIC SWAP gate — the
    // enqueuer that swaps false->true owns the (single) enqueue; the popper
    // stores false. Exactly-once membership without the old L_c section.
    queued: std.ArrayList(std.atomic.Value(bool)) = .empty,
    // N9-Q1/Q2: the run queues LIVE ON THE VM now (signal's wake-side enqueue
    // must reach them), one QLock each (rank l_q — never two held; steal pops
    // then owns with the pid in hand). `ready` remains only as the PRE-RUN
    // staging (boot/model spawns); driveThreads drains it into the home
    // queues single-threaded at start. `n_sched_live` == 0 means no engine is
    // running (model/boot paths stage into `ready`).
    run_qs: [SmpEngine.MAX_SCHED]std.ArrayList(Pid) = @splat(.empty),
    qlocks: [SmpEngine.MAX_SCHED]QLock = @splat(.{}),
    n_sched_live: std.atomic.Value(u32) = std.atomic.Value(u32).init(0),
    // N9-Q1: in-flight delivery guard — nonzero while a worker runs the parked
    // tick, so the atomics-only quiescence verdict cannot fire between a
    // delivery's flag-win and its queue append (the latent window the L_c
    // confirm used to narrow).
    delivering: std.atomic.Value(u32) = std.atomic.Value(u32).init(0),
    // gap-tracing-e7: the GLOBAL call-trace breakpoint table (see `TracePattern`).
    // Enabled predicates installed by `erlang:trace_pattern`; consulted by
    // `callPatternMatches` when a `call`-traced process traps a `.call_trace`
    // dispatch. Owns no term memory (atom indices only); freed in `deinit`.
    trace_patterns: std.ArrayList(TracePattern) = .empty,
    // gap-tracing-firepath: a stable literal region holding the installed trace
    // match specs (like registry's `region` / PersistentTerms) — never collected,
    // never moved after init; specs gcCopy INTO it at install, OUT at fire.
    trace_spec_region: FinalTerms.Ctx,
    // E8.2j/E8.2s-E8.2u: distribution state lives at the Vm boundary. Pending/live
    // peer rows span BIF calls; the local identity is installed only by
    // setnode/2 with a live registered net_kernel owner. Local pid/ref/port term
    // payloads still carry `nonode@nohost`; E8.2t/E8.2u synchronize derived
    // `Machine.dist_local_node`/creation observers so `node/1`, `term_to_binary`,
    // and `external_size` can see the active local identity without mutating
    // existing term payloads.
    dist_entries: std.ArrayList(DistEntry) = .empty,
    dist_node_monitors: std.ArrayList(DistNodeMonitor) = .empty,
    dist_remote_exits: std.ArrayList(DistRemoteExit) = .empty,
    dist_local_node: ?ta.AtomIdx = null,
    dist_local_creation: u32 = 0,
    dist_net_kernel: ?Pid = null,
    next_dist_conn: u64 = 1,
    // E6.2 (Task 2): the LIVE timer wheel + its per-entry semantic metadata, and
    // the VIRTUAL monotonic clock (ms) the wheel reads. `vclock` advances ONLY in
    // `advanceTimersToNext` (to the next pending deadline) — deterministic,
    // fuel/event-driven, never a wall-clock read, so every differential is
    // reproducible (the receive-after determinism requirement). `next_tref` mints
    // the TRef keys `send_after`/`start_timer` return and `cancel/read_timer` decode.
    timers: timer_wheel.WheelTimers = undefined, // init in Vm.init (needs the allocator)
    timer_meta: std.ArrayList(TimerMeta) = .empty,
    // e47-timer-meta-quadratic: a tref→idx map so `timerByTref` is O(1) instead of
    // a linear scan over ALL timer_meta (the O(n²) cancel/read path). Holds ONLY
    // live BIF-timer trefs (send_after/start_timer); receive timers use recv_timer.
    // put at arm, removed at cancel/fire — so map.count() == live BIF-timer count.
    timer_tref_idx: std.AutoHashMapUnmanaged(u64, usize) = .empty,
    vclock: u64 = 0,
    next_tref: u64 = 1,
    a_exit: ta.AtomIdx,
    a_down: ta.AtomIdx,
    a_process: ta.AtomIdx, //           E3.8-fix: the 'DOWN' tuple's Type element
    a_normal: ta.AtomIdx,
    a_killed: ta.AtomIdx,
    // E4.6 (Task 6): the captured stdout SINK — the printed-output evidence path.
    // A group-leader io fixture (`serviceGroupLeader`) appends `put_chars` bytes
    // here; `cli.runMulti` flushes it to real stdout ahead of the result line, so
    // `io:put_chars(...)`/`erlang:display/1` output is byte-comparable against the
    // OTP oracle (the NEW printed-output differential). Vm-owned; freed in deinit.
    io_output: std.ArrayList(u8) = .empty,
    // e50-halt: erlang:halt/1,2 requested node exit — `null` = running; set
    // = the OS exit code the driver (cli.runMulti) returns. `halt_slogan` is
    // the crashdump slogan (halt(String) captures it; a real node writes
    // erl_crash.dump — here it's the observable slogan + exit code 1).
    halt_code: ?u8 = null,
    halt_slogan: std.ArrayList(u8) = .empty,
    // E4.6: `get_line` input tape + read cursor (line-oriented). Empty by default
    // (an EOF tape), so `get_line` returns `eof` unless a driver seeds it.
    io_input: []const u8 = "",
    io_input_cursor: usize = 0,
    registry: registry.Registry, // E3.8: the name<->pid bijection (registry.zig),
    // now Vm-owned so `register/2`/`unregister/1`/`whereis/1`/
    // `process_info(_, registered_name)` can reach it. `terminate`/`normalExit`
    // call `registry.pidExited` — the DEATH law (a dead pid is unregistered) —
    // exactly the bijection registry.zig's own law suite already proves.
    // jit-wire-production: the PRODUCTION dispatch cache (engine #4, compile-once
    // per program identity). `grantOnly` runs each covered block as native
    // machine code (threaded fallback for the rest) through this cache instead of
    // the reference interpreter `ia.run` — a pure engine swap, `runProduction ≡
    // ia.run` (the whole-corpus law in dispatch.zig). Owns the compiled blocks +
    // their sealed W^X exec buffers; freed in `deinit`. Gated by
    // `dispatch.use_production_native` (default on; flip off for interpreter).
    prod_cache: dispatch.ProdCache = .{},
    ets: ea.EtsRegistry, // E7.3 (DIVERGENCE 143): the ONE shared ETS table space.
    // Vm-owned so every process sees the same tables (real cross-process ETS);
    // each Machine's `shared_ets` points here (set in `spawn`/`installGroupLeader`).
    // `terminate`/`normalExit` call `ets.ownerDied(pid)` — the E6.7 owner-death
    // lifecycle (heir transfer / heir-less auto-delete), now firing on real
    // process death. Owns its own `region` storage heap; freed in `deinit`.

    pub fn init(gpa: std.mem.Allocator, atoms: *AtomTable) !Vm {
        return .{
            .gpa = gpa,
            .atoms = atoms,
            .procs = .empty,
            .events = .empty,
            .a_exit = try atoms.intern("EXIT"),
            .a_down = try atoms.intern("DOWN"),
            .a_process = try atoms.intern("process"),
            .a_normal = try atoms.intern("normal"),
            .a_killed = try atoms.intern("killed"),
            .registry = registry.Registry.init(gpa),
            .ets = ea.EtsRegistry.init(gpa, atoms), // E7.3: the shared table space
            .timers = timer_wheel.WheelTimers.init(gpa), // E6.2: the live wheel
            .trace_spec_region = FinalTerms.Ctx.init(gpa, atoms), // gap-tracing-firepath
        };
    }

    pub fn deinit(self: *Vm) void {
        for (self.procs.items) |p| {
            p.machine.deinit();
            for (p.sigq.items) |*sm| self.freeSigFragment(sm); // e48-smp-s2: undrained fragments
            p.sigq.deinit(self.gpa);
            p.links.deinit(self.gpa);
            p.monitors_held.deinit(self.gpa);
            p.monitored_by.deinit(self.gpa);
            p.aliases.deinit(self.gpa); // E5.5: the process-local alias table
            self.gpa.destroy(p);
        }
        self.procs.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.stable_procs.deinit(self.gpa);
        // gap-b-5a: the relocated `L_c`-domain per-pid arrays + the ready inbox +
        // the deliverParked snapshot scratch (all own no term memory — plain
        // scalars/pids — so just the backing storage is freed).
        self.owned.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.pending_wake_a.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.suspend_a.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.ready.deinit(self.gpa);
        for (&self.run_qs) |*q| q.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.queued.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.events.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.io_output.deinit(self.gpa); // E4.6: the captured stdout sink
        self.halt_slogan.deinit(self.gpa); // e50-halt
        self.registry.deinit();
        self.ets.deinit(); // E7.3: the shared ETS table space + its region heap
        // E5.4: reap every live child (idempotent — a closed port is already
        // reaped) so no OS process or fd outlives the Vm.
        for (self.ports.items) |*rp| {
            rp.live.deinit();
            self.gpa.free(rp.name); // E6.7: the owned command-name bytes
        }
        self.ports.deinit(self.gpa);
        // gap-socket-real-tcp: close every live socket fd (idempotent) so no OS
        // socket outlives the Vm — zero-leak over the whole table.
        for (self.sockets.items) |*rs| {
            if (rs.alive) socket_algebra.close(rs.fd);
            rs.accept_waiters.deinit(self.gpa); // gap-accept-waiters-queue: zero-leak
        }
        self.sockets.deinit(self.gpa);
        // gap-file-handle-io: close every live file handle (idempotent) so no OS fd
        // outlives the Vm (FMEA FM-FD-1). A handle can only exist if `fs` was set.
        if (self.fs) |fs| {
            for (self.fds.items) |*rf| {
                if (rf.alive) prim_file.close(fs.io, rf.handle);
            }
        }
        self.fds.deinit(self.gpa);
        self.parked_spawn.deinit(self.gpa); // E5.6: reply terms live in proc heaps
        self.parked_systask.deinit(self.gpa); // E7.5: reply terms live in proc heaps
        self.staged_spawn.deinit(self.gpa); // gap-b-3: staging owns no term memory
        self.staged_systask.deinit(self.gpa); // gap-b-3: staging owns no term memory
        self.trace_patterns.deinit(self.gpa); // gap-tracing-e7: atom indices only
        self.trace_spec_region.deinit(); // gap-tracing-firepath: the stable spec region
        self.dist_entries.deinit(self.gpa); // E8.2j: no term ownership
        self.dist_node_monitors.deinit(self.gpa); // E8.2q: no term ownership
        for (self.dist_remote_exits.items) |e| self.gpa.free(e.reason_wire);
        self.dist_remote_exits.deinit(self.gpa); // E8.2y: owns reason_wire bytes
        self.timers.deinit(); // E6.2: the live timer wheel
        self.timer_meta.deinit(self.gpa); // timer msg/dest terms live in proc heaps
        self.timer_tref_idx.deinit(self.gpa); // e47-timer-meta-quadratic: the tref→idx index
        self.prod_cache.deinit(self.gpa); // jit-wire-production: compiled blocks + W^X exec buffers
    }

    /// gap-b-5b: the ONE writer of `Vm.events`. Appends `ev` under `L_events`
    /// (the LOWEST leaf lock) so concurrent appenders never lose/tear an event
    /// and the log order is linearizable. BY CONSTRUCTION no back-edge: this body
    /// references only `self.l_events`, `self.events`, `self.gpa`, and `ev` — it
    /// holds NO reference to any higher lock (`L_v`/`L_c`/`L_reg`/`L_timer`/
    /// `L_e`), so it CANNOT acquire one while holding `L_events`. Callers invoke
    /// it EITHER holding a higher lock (the sanctioned down-edge, e.g. the SMP
    /// grant epilogue under `L_v`) OR lock-free (single-thread `grant`/
    /// `driveModel`); the append order is UNCHANGED from the pre-slice direct
    /// `self.events.append`, so `denote` is preserved.
    pub fn emitEvent(self: *Vm, ev: Event) !void {
        // N7 (DIVERGENCE 621): a WORKER thread appends to its OWN shard with a
        // lock-free seq stamp — no `L_events` acquire on the hot emit path.
        // The shard is merged (seq-sorted) into `events` at the driveThreads
        // join, reproducing a valid linearization of the old locked appends
        // (each emit's linearization point is the `event_seq` fetchAdd).
        // Non-worker emitters (driveModel/grant/boot/tests) keep the classic
        // locked global append — byte-identical behavior.
        if (tl_event_buf) |buf| {
            const seq = self.event_seq.fetchAdd(1, .monotonic);
            try buf.append(self.gpa, .{ .seq = seq, .ev = ev });
            return;
        }
        self.l_events.lock();
        defer self.l_events.unlock();
        try self.events.append(self.gpa, ev);
    }

    /// gap-fs-autoloader: the Vm-level autoload configuration. `code_path`
    /// entries are directory SUB-PATHS under `root`, searched in order —
    /// the first `<dir>/<Module>.beam` that reads wins (erts code-path order).
    pub const AutoloadCfg = struct {
        io: std.Io,
        root: std.Io.Dir,
        code_path: []const []const u8,
    };

    /// gap-erl-cli-args (DIVERGENCE 598): a proc-native view of the parsed init argv
    /// (mirrors cli.InitFlag/InitArgs; kept here so proc does not import cli — the
    /// run path converts). `flags` is one entry per `-flag` OCCURRENCE (a flag may
    /// repeat); `plain` is the `-extra`/bare tokens. All slices borrow argv bytes.
    pub const InitArg = struct { name: []const u8, values: []const []const u8 };
    pub const InitArgsView = struct { flags: []const InitArg, plain: []const []const u8 };

    /// gap-erl-cli-args: the init flags that `init` CONSUMES — `init:get_argument/1`
    /// returns `error` for these even when present (they are boot/emulator
    /// directives, not user arguments), matching pinned OTP-30 (probed: s/run/eval/
    /// boot/config/name → error; pa/pz/user-flags → {ok,_}). `-pa`/`-pz` (code path)
    /// stay RETRIEVABLE. This is the observable consumed set; the uncommon tail
    /// (mode/root/home/boot_var/…) is the documented bound.
    pub fn initFlagConsumed(name: []const u8) bool {
        const consumed = [_][]const u8{ "s", "run", "eval", "boot", "boot_var", "config", "mode", "root", "home", "name", "sname", "extra", "epmd_module", "proto_dist" };
        for (consumed) |c| if (std.mem.eql(u8, name, c)) return true;
        return false;
    }

    /// e49-wire-real-file-io: the real-file seam config — an Io + a root Dir,
    /// the same two capabilities prim_file's E4.4 laws use. file:read_file/
    /// write_file resolve paths UNDER this root (no escape — prim_file's
    /// sub_path semantics).
    pub const FsCfg = struct {
        io: std.Io,
        root: std.Io.Dir,
    };

    /// e49-wire-real-file-io: the byte cap on a file:read_file (64 MiB — the
    /// same generous bound the autoloader uses; a larger file → {error,enomem}).
    const file_read_max: usize = 64 << 20;

    /// gap-fs-autoloader: the byte cap on an autoloaded beam read (the same
    /// generous 64 MiB bound `zigvm_main` uses for CLI-supplied beams).
    const autoload_read_max: usize = 64 << 20;

    /// e47-a3 (CTRL-AUTOLOAD UCA-AL-2'): the autoload DENYLIST. Auto-loading
    /// stock `error_handler.beam` LIVELOCKS the node (its undefined_function
    /// hook calls code:ensure_loaded → undef → hook → …, the E46 gap
    /// analysis's empirical trap); stock logger/error_logger pull the whole
    /// logger tree the VM deliberately does not run (the e47-a1 disclosed
    /// bound). These three resolve NATIVELY (error_handler via the built-in
    /// undef path; logger:allow/error_logger:get_format_depth via
    /// resolveLibrary) — loading the stock beams over them would REPLACE a
    /// working native surface with a livelocking one. A denylisted miss falls
    /// through to error_handler/undef exactly like a not-found module.
    pub fn autoloadDenied(name: []const u8) bool {
        const deny = [_][]const u8{ "error_handler", "logger", "error_logger" };
        for (deny) |d| {
            if (std.mem.eql(u8, name, d)) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    pub fn spawn(self: *Vm, prog: ia.Program, entry: u32, arg: ?i64) !Pid {
        const p = try self.gpa.create(Process);
        errdefer self.gpa.destroy(p);
        p.* = .{
            .machine = try ia.Machine.init(self.gpa, self.atoms),
            .prog = prog,
            .sigq = .empty,
            .links = .empty,
            .monitors_held = .empty,
            .monitored_by = .empty,
            .aliases = .empty,
        };
        p.machine.pc = entry;
        p.machine.shared_ets = &self.ets; // E7.3: every process shares the Vm's ETS space
        // gap-fs-autoloader: every process of an autoloading Vm may trap
        // `ensure_loaded`; without a config the flag stays false (byte-identical).
        p.machine.autoload_enabled = self.autoload != null;
        // gap-dynamic-native-dispatch (DIVERGENCE 650): a runtime M:F/A dispatch
        // resolves the native bif surface (resolve ∪ resolveLibrary) as a fallback
        // above error_handler/undef, so a dynamic apply/Mod:Var to a wired wrapper
        // works like a static call_ext (fixes the FM-DISPATCH-DEAD-via-apply class).
        p.machine.native_bif_resolver = &bif_dispatch.resolveDynamic;
        // gap-fs-autoloader: the static/dynamic pc split must sit at THIS
        // process's static program length, or a runtime load (autoload /
        // finish_loading from a non-entry process) would splice its code onto
        // pc 0 and shadow the static program. `cli.runMulti` re-sets the entry
        // machine's split to the same value (linked.prog.len) — consistent.
        p.machine.code_base = prog.len;
        const pid: Pid = @intCast(self.procs.items.len);
        p.machine.self_pid = pid;
        if (self.distLocalActive()) {
            p.machine.dist_local_node = self.dist_local_node;
            p.machine.dist_local_creation = self.dist_local_creation;
        }
        // E3.8: a fresh top-level spawn is its OWN group leader (erts' default
        // for a process with no parent to inherit from). Callers that DO know a
        // parent (interpret's `.spawn`/`.spawn_fun`/`.spawn_mfa`/`.spawn_request`
        // arms) overwrite this with the parent's group leader right after.
        p.machine.group_leader = pid;
        // spawn-argument convention: x15 = argument, x14 = own pid
        if (arg) |a| p.machine.regs[15] = FinalTerms.int(&p.machine.ctx, a);
        p.machine.regs[14] = FinalTerms.int(&p.machine.ctx, pid);
        try self.procs.append(self.gpa, p);
        // gap-b-5a/gap-b-6a: grow the `L_c`-domain per-pid arrays in LOCKSTEP with
        // `procs` (pid == index). errdefer-shrink `procs` so a mid-grow OOM leaves all
        // arrays the same length (no torn pid index). These appends happen under the
        // caller's `L_v` (interpret spawn arms) or lock-free (boot) — never inside an
        // `L_c` section, so no reentrancy; the coordinator's pid-index reads are then
        // pointer-stable. (No `L_c` needed at THIS step: `L_v` still serializes spawn
        // vs the coordinator; gap-b-5 wraps these in `L_c` when it narrows `L_v`.)
        errdefer _ = self.procs.pop();
        // N4: dual-write the stable mirror (same box, same index) AFTER the
        // procs errdefer is armed, with its own count rollback: any spawn-OOM
        // unwind leaves mirror == procs exactly (a shrunk count's stale slot
        // is dead: readers bound on count).
        try self.stable_procs.append(self.gpa, p);
        errdefer self.stable_procs.count.store(self.stable_procs.count.load(.monotonic) - 1, .release);
        try self.owned.append(self.gpa, std.atomic.Value(u32).init(NO_OWNER));
        errdefer _ = self.owned.pop();
        try self.pending_wake_a.append(self.gpa, std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false));
        errdefer _ = self.pending_wake_a.pop();
        try self.suspend_a.append(self.gpa, std.atomic.Value(u32).init(0)); // gap-b-6a: fresh proc unsuspended
        errdefer _ = self.suspend_a.pop();
        try self.queued.append(self.gpa, std.atomic.Value(bool).init(false));
        errdefer _ = self.queued.pop();
        // gap-b-5a: PUSH the fresh proc onto the ready inbox if it is schedulable —
        // this is the spawn half of push-based discovery (covers BOTH the initial
        // entry proc, spawned at boot before `driveThreads`, and every runtime
        // spawn in `interpret`). `markSchedulable` dedups via `queued`, so a proc
        // that boot pushed and a later re-check are idempotent.
        try self.markSchedulable(pid);
        return pid;
    }

    fn proc(self: *Vm, pid: Pid) ?*Process {
        if (pid >= self.procs.items.len) return null;
        const p = self.procs.items[pid];
        return if (p.alive) p else null;
    }

    /// E4.1: a spawned child inherits the parent's LINKED code space — the
    /// static export index (`m.exports`, cross-module `call_ext_code`), the
    /// cooked-stacktrace location table (`m.locs`), and the combined literal
    /// pool (`m.literals`). All three are BORROWED read-only slices owned by the
    /// driver (`cli.runMulti`/`boot`), never freed by a Machine, so sharing the
    /// pointer is a pure aliasing — the child can call any exported function and
    /// reference any literal the entry process can. Without this a child running
    /// real compiled code would raise `undef`/read past an empty literal table.
    pub fn inheritCode(self: *Vm, child: Pid, parent: *ia.Machine) !void {
        const cm = &self.procs.items[child].machine;
        cm.exports = parent.exports;
        cm.locs = parent.locs;
        // e21-t2b (DIVERGENCE 510): literals are ctx-LOCAL — a compound literal
        // (tuple/list/map/bignum) is a boxed term whose words index the heap that
        // MATERIALIZED it. Borrowing `parent.literals` (the pre-e21-t2b bug) let a
        // child resolve `.literal i` to a parent-ctx boxed word and then read it in
        // its OWN ctx: OOB / mis-tagged garbage (the spawned-child `{r,99}` send
        // that panicked `bigParts`/`listHead`, or silently produced a non-matching
        // tuple → receive deadlock). Give the child its OWN table, each entry
        // gcCopy'd cross-heap into the child's ctx (immediates copy verbatim;
        // compounds are deep-rebased) — the same copy law `send` uses. Owned →
        // freed in `Machine.deinit` (`owns_literals`). Empty table stays the shared
        // `&.{}` sentinel (no alloc).
        if (parent.literals.len == 0) {
            cm.literals = parent.literals;
        } else {
            const owned = try self.gpa.alloc(FinalTerms.Term, parent.literals.len);
            for (parent.literals, 0..) |lit, i|
                owned[i] = try FinalTerms.gcCopy(&cm.ctx, &parent.ctx, lit);
            cm.literals = owned;
            cm.owns_literals = true;
            // e48-gc-literal-area: the child's literal copies are a heap
            // PREFIX (fresh ctx + inherit right after init) — set its GC
            // water line so a collect keeps them byte-stable in place.
            cm.heap_lit_end = cm.ctx.words.items.len;
        }
    }

    /// E4.1: resolve a `spawn(M, F, ArgList)` MFA against the child's inherited
    /// export index and, on a hit, unpack `ArgList` into the child's
    /// x0..x[arity-1] (each element gcCopy'd cross-heap from the parent), then
    /// return the entry pc. `null` on: a non-atom M/F, an improper/over-long arg
    /// list, or an MFA no loaded module exports. Bounded (arg count capped at 16,
    /// the x-register window). The child and parent share the global atom table,
    /// so `atomIdxOf` gives the same index `m.exports` was built with.
    /// gap-fs-autoloader: load `<code_path>/<Module>.beam` into `target`'s code
    /// space (the child's, on a spawn), returning whether it now resolves. The
    /// process-birth analog of the `.ensure_loaded` dispatch-miss autoload — a
    /// spawn target not yet loaded (real-app dep tree under `--code-path`) is
    /// loaded here instead of failing CLOSED to `undef`. Best-effort: no autoload
    /// cfg / denied / not-found / bad-beam → false (the caller renders `undef`).
    fn autoloadModuleInto(self: *Vm, target: *ia.Machine, module: ta.AtomIdx) !bool {
        const al = self.autoload orelse return false;
        const name = target.ctx.atoms.nameOf(module);
        if (autoloadDenied(name)) return false;
        for (al.code_path) |dir| {
            var pbuf: [512]u8 = undefined;
            const path = std.fmt.bufPrint(&pbuf, "{s}/{s}.beam", .{ dir, name }) catch return false;
            const bytes = prim_file.readFile(al.io, al.root, self.gpa, path, autoload_read_max) catch |e| switch (e) {
                error.OutOfMemory => return error.OutOfMemory,
                else => continue, // Enoent/short/… → next code_path dir
            };
            defer self.gpa.free(bytes);
            code_bifs.loadBeamBytes(target, module, bytes) catch |e| switch (e) {
                error.OutOfMemory => return error.OutOfMemory,
                error.Badfile, error.OnLoad => return false, // a bad find is a miss
            };
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    pub fn resolveSpawnMFA(
        self: *Vm,
        cp: *Process,
        m: *ia.Machine,
        module: FinalTerms.Term,
        func: FinalTerms.Term,
        arglist: FinalTerms.Term,
    ) ?u32 {
        if (!FinalTerms.repIsAtom(module) or !FinalTerms.repIsAtom(func)) return null;
        // count the (proper, bounded) argument list
        var n: u32 = 0;
        var cur = arglist;
        while (FinalTerms.kindOf(&m.ctx, cur) == .cons) {
            n += 1;
            if (n > 16) return null;
            cur = FinalTerms.listTail(&m.ctx, cur);
        }
        if (FinalTerms.kindOf(&m.ctx, cur) != .nil) return null; // improper list
        const mod_idx = FinalTerms.atomIdxOf(module);
        const func_idx = FinalTerms.atomIdxOf(func);
        // gap-procinfo-initial-call (DIVERGENCE 754): record the spawn MFA at birth so
        // `process_info(cp, initial_call)` returns {M, F, length(Args)} (OTP PCB parity).
        // Set from the resolved atom indices + the counted arity `n`, BEFORE code
        // resolution — erts stamps `initial_call` at spawn even for an MFA that then
        // exits `undef`. Purely additive; touches no scheduler/signal state.
        cp.initial_call = .{ .m = mod_idx, .f = func_idx, .a = n };
        // gap-fs-autoloader: resolve the spawn target through the CHILD's RUNTIME
        // (dyn/autoloaded) code first, then its static exports — and if neither has
        // it yet, AUTOLOAD it into the child (the birth analog of dispatchMFA's
        // ensure_loaded-then-retry), so a real-app process tree spawned under
        // `--code-path` starts instead of dying `undef`.
        var pc = cp.machine.resolveRuntime(mod_idx, func_idx, n) orelse
            cp.machine.resolveExport(mod_idx, func_idx, n);
        if (pc == null and (self.autoloadModuleInto(&cp.machine, mod_idx) catch false)) {
            pc = cp.machine.resolveRuntime(mod_idx, func_idx, n) orelse
                cp.machine.resolveExport(mod_idx, func_idx, n);
        }
        const resolved = pc orelse return null;
        // unpack args into the child's low x-registers (cross-heap copy)
        cur = arglist;
        var i: usize = 0;
        while (FinalTerms.kindOf(&m.ctx, cur) == .cons) : (i += 1) {
            const head = FinalTerms.listHead(&m.ctx, cur);
            cp.machine.regs[i] = FinalTerms.gcCopy(&cp.machine.ctx, &m.ctx, head) catch return null;
            cur = FinalTerms.listTail(&m.ctx, cur);
        }
        return resolved;
    }

    /// Resolve a term that NAMES a process to its `proc` index. E3.7 makes this
    /// TOTAL over BOTH pid encodings: a real pid TERM (Task 5) — its `number`
    /// field IS the proc index (the law-covered pid↔process mapping) — and the
    /// LEGACY small-int pid the M7 engine/law suite uses (ring/G3 programs put an
    /// int in x14 and send to int targets). Accepting both is what keeps every
    /// pre-E3.7 proc law green while pid TERMs flow through the same bridge. A
    /// term that is neither (garbage / a dead-pid number out of range) → null,
    /// and the caller DROPS the signal — never a crash, never a UAF (§10).
    fn pidOfTerm(ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx, w: FinalTerms.Term) ?Pid {
        if (FinalTerms.repIsPid(ctx, w)) return std.math.cast(Pid, FinalTerms.pidNumber(ctx, w));
        if (!FinalTerms.repIsSmall(w)) return null;
        const v = @as(i64, @bitCast(w)) >> 4;
        if (v < 0) return null;
        return std.math.cast(Pid, v);
    }

    fn pidTermIsLocal(ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx, w: FinalTerms.Term) bool {
        return FinalTerms.repIsPid(ctx, w) and
            FinalTerms.pidCreation(ctx, w) == 0 and
            std.mem.eql(u8, FinalTerms.pidNodeName(ctx, w), ta.local_node_name);
    }

    fn refTermIsLocal(ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx, w: FinalTerms.Term) bool {
        return FinalTerms.repIsRef(ctx, w) and
            FinalTerms.refCreation(ctx, w) == 0 and
            std.mem.eql(u8, FinalTerms.refNodeName(ctx, w), ta.local_node_name);
    }

    /// Resolve an EXIT-signal identity. Unlike `send/2`, an exit signal must never
    /// let a foreign ETF pid/ref's numeric fields alias a local process or alias ref.
    /// Local pids route by pid number; local refs route only if they are active
    /// aliases; foreign pid/ref terms are no-carrier drops; legacy small ints keep
    /// the pre-E3 proc laws alive.
    fn resolveExitTarget(self: *Vm, ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx, w: FinalTerms.Term) ?Pid {
        if (FinalTerms.repIsRef(ctx, w)) {
            if (!refTermIsLocal(ctx, w)) return null;
            return self.resolveAliasOwner(keyFromRefWords(FinalTerms.refWords(ctx, w)));
        }
        if (FinalTerms.repIsPid(ctx, w)) {
            if (!pidTermIsLocal(ctx, w)) return null;
            return std.math.cast(Pid, FinalTerms.pidNumber(ctx, w));
        }
        if (!FinalTerms.repIsSmall(w)) return null;
        const v = @as(i64, @bitCast(w)) >> 4;
        if (v < 0) return null;
        return std.math.cast(Pid, v);
    }

    /// E3.8-fix: monitor identity IS a genuine E3.5 boxed ref (`FinalTerms.ref`),
    /// not a disguised small int — a compiled `receive {'DOWN', R, process, P,
    /// Reason} -> ...` must match a REAL ref. Monitors are still bookkept
    /// internally by a u32-fit u64 key (`next_ref`, unchanged), but that key is
    /// now round-tripped through a ref TERM's words at every boundary: created
    /// once in the monitoring process's heap (`monitor_to`), reconstructed with
    /// the SAME words in the watcher's heap when the 'DOWN' fires (refs are
    /// content-addressed — `eqlExact`/`cmpRef` compare words, not pointer
    /// identity — so re-`FinalTerms.ref`-ing the same words in a different Ctx
    /// yields an observably-equal ref), and decoded back to the key whenever a
    /// caller passes a ref term into `demonitor`/`demonitor(...,[flush])`.
    fn refWordsForKey(key: u64) [3]u32 {
        return .{ 0, @truncate(key >> 32), @truncate(key) };
    }
    fn keyFromRefWords(words: [3]u32) u64 {
        return (@as(u64, words[1]) << 32) | @as(u64, words[2]);
    }

    /// E5.5 (Task 5): find the process that owns an ACTIVE alias with ref `key`,
    /// or null (no such active alias — the send is DROPPED, exactly the erts
    /// retired-alias behavior). A dead owner is skipped (its aliases are
    /// implicitly retired), so owner-death retirement needs no per-entry work.
    /// Bounded (procs × that proc's alias count, both bounded). Never a UAF (§10).
    fn resolveAliasOwner(self: *Vm, key: u64) ?Pid {
        for (self.procs.items, 0..) |p, i| {
            if (!p.alive) continue;
            for (p.aliases.items) |a| {
                if (a.key == key and a.active) return @intCast(i);
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    /// e50-eep76: is the destination term a REF naming a PRIORITY alias? A
    /// priority-alias send lands at the FRONT of the owner's mailbox (EEP-76).
    /// Bounded exactly like `resolveAliasOwner`; never a UAF.
    fn sendTargetIsPriority(self: *Vm, ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx, w: FinalTerms.Term) bool {
        if (!FinalTerms.repIsRef(ctx, w)) return false;
        const key = keyFromRefWords(FinalTerms.refWords(ctx, w));
        for (self.procs.items) |p| {
            if (!p.alive) continue;
            for (p.aliases.items) |a| {
                if (a.key == key and a.active) return a.priority;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    /// E5.5: resolve a `!/2` destination term to a target proc index. A pid (or
    /// the legacy small-int pid) routes by `pidOfTerm`; a REFERENCE routes to the
    /// owner of the active alias it names (`resolveAliasOwner`) — the alias
    /// delivery law. Anything else / an inactive alias → null (dropped).
    fn resolveSendTarget(self: *Vm, ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx, w: FinalTerms.Term) ?Pid {
        if (FinalTerms.repIsRef(ctx, w)) return self.resolveAliasOwner(keyFromRefWords(FinalTerms.refWords(ctx, w)));
        // E5.8 (Task 8, DIVERGENCE entry 50(a)): a registered-NAME destination
        // (`Name ! Msg`) routes to the pid `Name` is bound to in the registry —
        // the same name<->pid bijection `whereis/1` reads. An unregistered name
        // resolves to null (dropped, the model's dead/absent-target behavior);
        // a live registered pid delivers. This is the ONLY missing arm — the
        // `send` opcode already traps `.send_to` with the atom in x0.
        if (FinalTerms.repIsAtom(w)) return self.registry.whereis(FinalTerms.atomIdxOf(w));
        if (FinalTerms.repIsPid(ctx, w) and !pidTermIsLocal(ctx, w)) {
            if (std.math.cast(Pid, FinalTerms.pidNumber(ctx, w))) |pid| {
                return 0x80000000 | pid;
            } else return null;
        }
        return pidOfTerm(ctx, w);
    }

    /// E5.8 (Task 8): the spawn-result term. A plain spawn returns the child's
    /// pid TERM; a `[monitor]` spawn (`spawn_opt(_,_,_,[monitor])` /
    /// `spawn_monitor/1,3`) mints a genuine E3.5 boxed ref, installs the monitor
    /// (`monitors_held` on the spawner, `monitored_by` on the child — the SAME
    /// bidirectional bookkeeping `monitor/2` uses, so the child's later 'DOWN'
    /// fires through the ordinary signal path), and returns `{Pid, Ref}` — the
    /// erlang.erl `spawn_monitor` wrapper's shape. DIVERGENCE entry 50(c).
    fn spawnResult(self: *Vm, p: *Process, m: *ia.Machine, pid: Pid, child: Pid, monitor: bool) !FinalTerms.Term {
        const child_pid = try FinalTerms.pid(&m.ctx, child, 0);
        if (!monitor) return child_pid;
        const ref = self.next_ref;
        self.next_ref += 1;
        const ref_term = try FinalTerms.ref(&m.ctx, refWordsForKey(ref));
        try p.monitors_held.append(self.gpa, .{ .ref = ref, .target = child });
        try self.procs.items[child].monitored_by.append(self.gpa, .{ .ref = ref, .watcher = pid });
        return try FinalTerms.tuple(&m.ctx, &.{ child_pid, ref_term });
    }

    /// Send one signal A→B, stamped for the per-pair order law. Payload
    /// terms must already live in B's heap (message copy happens in the
    /// action interpreter, where the copy law is measured).
    pub fn signal(self: *Vm, from: Pid, to: Pid, sig: Signal) !void {
        return self.signalInner(from, to, sig, null);
    }

    /// sender-narrowing N1: `signal` with an optionally PRECOMPUTED send-event
    /// vhash. `pre_vhash == null` → compute as before (the direct paths, where
    /// the payload heap is quiescent under the caller's `L_v`). The parked
    /// delivery paths pass the park-time hash so delivery never reads the
    /// requester's heap (it may be running unlocked on another worker).
    fn signalInner(self: *Vm, from: Pid, to: Pid, sig: Signal, pre_vhash: ?u64) !void {
        const target = self.proc(to) orelse return; // dead/absent: dropped
        const stamp = self.next_stamp.fetchAdd(1, .monotonic); // e48-smp-s2 atomic mint
        // N4 ORDER FIX (DIVERGENCE 618, TSan-caught UAF): the send-event vhash
        // is computed BEFORE the sigq append. Once appended, a `.message_frag`
        // belongs to the receiver — whose drain now runs fully OFF `L_v` and
        // may consume + FREE the fragment before this sender's next line (the
        // old post-append hash was safe only because the detach used to wait
        // on this sender's `L_v` hold). Pre-append, the fragment is still
        // sender-owned; the direct `.message` hash reads the target heap under
        // this sender's `L_v` as before.
        const send_vhash: ?u64 = switch (sig) {
            .message => |payload| pre_vhash orelse FinalTerms.hashTerm(&target.machine.ctx, payload),
            .message_frag => |frag| pre_vhash orelse FinalTerms.hashTerm(&frag.ctx, frag.root),
            else => null,
        };
        // S-epoch Option B: the append is `L_sigq`-guarded — self-synchronized
        // against the receiver's off-`L_v` batch detach in `drainSignals`,
        // independent of whether this sender holds `L_v`. Leaf section: the
        // append alone, no lock reach (the wake decision below is `L_c`, taken
        // AFTER `L_sigq` is released — in-order, never nested inside it).
        {
            target.sigq_lock.lock();
            defer target.sigq_lock.unlock();
            try target.sigq.append(self.gpa, .{ .from = from, .stamp = stamp, .sig = sig });
        }
        // E3.7 RECEIVE-WAKE (upgrades DIVERGENCE entry 5's terminal-`wait` model
        // for the multi-process case): a process blocked in the E1.11 receive loop
        // sits at `.suspended` (`wait`/`wait_timeout infinity` set it, with pc at
        // the `loop_rec` label and the save-pointer reset). Enqueuing a signal for
        // it makes it RUNNABLE again — the scheduler grants it, `drainSignals`
        // delivers this signal (in stamp order, so the sacred per-pair order is
        // UNTOUCHED), and the woken process re-enters `loop_rec` and finds the new
        // message. Waking here (not at delivery) is what lets `liveCount` see it so
        // the scheduler schedules it; if NO signal ever arrives it stays suspended
        // and the run reaches quiescence (bounded — a hang is a failed law). The
        // E6.2 (Task 2): the real receive TIMER is now LIVE — `wait_timeout` finite
        // arms a wheel entry (`recv_timer`). A message arriving BEFORE the timeout
        // CANCELS that entry EXACTLY-ONCE (the lost-wakeup guard's timer half): the
        // `recv_timer != null` test fires the cancel once and clears the handle, so
        // a later wheel advance never delivers a spurious `timeout` to a process
        // that already received a message (cancel-then-fire-impossible at the live
        // layer). A `.suspended` process with no armed timer (`wait`/`infinity`)
        // is unaffected.
        // E4.6: a group-leader io fixture is PASSIVE — enqueuing an io_request
        // must NOT wake it into the scheduler (it has no code to run); it is
        // serviced explicitly by `serviceGroupLeader`. Every other process wakes
        // as before (the E3.7 receive-wake).
        // e48-smp-s3: an OWNED target's wake is DEFERRED — the owning worker
        // applies it at release under the lock (racing the owner's unlocked
        // status writes is the lost-wakeup/torn-write hazard this closes; a
        // signal that lands after the owner's last drain but before its
        // suspend is exactly the case pending_wake preserves).
        // gap-b-5a: the wake DECISION (owned-check + defer-or-wake-and-enqueue) is
        // ONE `L_c` section — `owned`/`pending_wake_a`/`ready`/`queued` are all
        // coherency-domain. It is atomic w.r.t. the worker's mark-owned (which
        // sets `owned`/clears `queued` under `L_c`): so a signal never enqueues a
        // pid the worker is about to own (→ deferred via `pending_wake_a`) nor
        // double-enqueues an already-queued pid. `applyWake→timers.cancel` takes
        // `L_timer` (BELOW `L_c`, sanctioned down-edge); no site under this `L_c`
        // re-takes `L_c` (deliverParked no longer holds `L_c` across `signal`).
        {
            // N9-Q1 DEKKER (SC-N9-1): announce the wake FIRST (.seq_cst), then
            // re-read ownership. Both sides store-then-check, so at least one
            // observes the other: the flag is consumed by EXACTLY one winner
            // (here, or the owner's release), and a wake is never lost.
            // acq_rel is INSUFFICIENT (store->load visibility) — the Quint
            // model specs/lc_wake_release.qnt checks this exact protocol.
            self.pending_wake_a.items[to].store(true, .seq_cst);
            if (comptime @import("builtin").is_test) std.Thread.yield() catch {}; // widen the Dekker window
            if (self.owned.items[to].load(.seq_cst) == NO_OWNER) {
                if (self.pending_wake_a.items[to].swap(false, .seq_cst)) {
                    self.applyWake(target);
                    try self.enqueueDirect(to);
                }
            }
        }
        // e48-smp-s3 (S2 gap closed): a fragment message emits the SAME send
        // event — the pre-append fragment-heap vhash denotes exactly what the
        // receiver-side merge will (hashTerm is denotational).
        if (send_vhash) |vh| {
            try self.emitEvent(.{ .send = .{
                .from = from,
                .to = to,
                .stamp = stamp,
                .vhash = vh,
            } });
        }
    }

    /// e48-smp-s3: the RECEIVE-WAKE (factored from `signal`) — wake a
    /// suspended non-io_gl process and cancel its armed receive timer
    /// exactly-once. Called by `signal` for un-owned targets and by the
    /// worker's release epilogue for deferred (`pending_wake`) ones.
    fn applyWake(self: *Vm, target: *Process) void {
        if (target.machine.status == .suspended and !target.io_gl) {
            target.machine.status = .running;
            if (target.recv_timer) |idx| {
                if (idx < self.timer_meta.items.len and self.timer_meta.items[idx].live) {
                    _ = self.timers.cancel(self.timer_meta.items[idx].wheel_id);
                    self.timer_meta.items[idx].live = false;
                }
                target.recv_timer = null;
            }
        }
    }

    // ---- gap-b-5a (SMP step 5a): PUSH-BASED discovery — the `L_c`-domain ready
    // inbox + the exactly-once `queued` dedup bit. These three helpers replace the
    // worker's `procs`-scanning `discover` in the hot coordinator path. ----

    /// N9-Q1 (DIVERGENCE 623): the direct enqueue — replaces the old global
    /// `L_c` `enqueueReadyLocked`/`drainReady` pair with the pid's existing
    /// home-queue `QLock`. Guards, in order: bounds; a fast OWNED observation;
    /// then, under the same QLock used by every pop, an OWNED recheck and the
    /// DEDUP SWAP. The enqueuer that flips `queued` false→true owns the single
    /// enqueue (exactly-once membership). The under-lock recheck closes the
    /// SC-N9-2 stale-observation window between a pop and its ownership CAS.
    /// The `runnable_total` hint is bumped BEFORE the append (a transient
    /// over-count only delays quiescence; an under-count could falsify it).
    /// With no live engine (`n_sched_live == 0` — model/boot) the pid stages
    /// into `ready`, drained single-threaded by `driveThreads` at start.
    fn enqueueAfterUnownedObservation(self: *Vm, pid: Pid, n: u32) !void {
        // N9 TSan fix: NO status/liveness reads here — `alive`/`machine.status`
        // belong to whoever owns/runs the proc, and this path holds no claim on
        // it (the pre-N9 L_c section did). A spurious enqueue of a dead or
        // suspended pid is BENIGN: the pick's post-CAS check (owner-safe by
        // then) absorbs it with a mini-release, no grant emitted.
        if (n == 0) {
            if (self.queued.items[pid].swap(true, .seq_cst)) return; // single-threaded boot dedup
            _ = self.runnable_total.fetchAdd(1, .monotonic); // BEFORE the append (see doc)
            self.ready.append(self.gpa, pid) catch |e| {
                self.queued.items[pid].store(false, .seq_cst);
                _ = self.runnable_total.fetchSub(1, .monotonic);
                return e;
            };
            return;
        }
        const h = SmpEngine.homeOf(pid, n);
        self.qlocks[h].lock();
        defer self.qlocks[h].unlock();
        // SC-N9-2: the caller's owner observation may be stale. A popper removes
        // under this same QLock before claiming ownership and clearing queued;
        // rechecking here makes that transition indivisible from a new
        // membership reservation without adding another lock acquisition.
        if (self.owned.items[pid].load(.seq_cst) != NO_OWNER) return;
        if (self.queued.items[pid].swap(true, .seq_cst)) return;
        _ = self.runnable_total.fetchAdd(1, .monotonic); // BEFORE the append (see doc)
        self.run_qs[h].append(self.gpa, pid) catch |e| {
            self.queued.items[pid].store(false, .seq_cst);
            _ = self.runnable_total.fetchSub(1, .monotonic);
            return e;
        };
    }

    fn enqueueDirect(self: *Vm, pid: Pid) !void {
        if (pid >= self.procs.items.len) return; // bounds (== owned/queued/suspend_a len)
        if (self.owned.items[pid].load(.seq_cst) != NO_OWNER) return; // owner requeues at release
        const n = self.n_sched_live.load(.acquire);
        try self.enqueueAfterUnownedObservation(pid, n);
    }

    /// N9-Q1: wake sites that are not the Dekker signal path (`spawn`,
    /// timers, `resume_process`, group-leader service) — a thin alias now
    /// that enqueue coordination is local to the pid's home queue.
    fn markSchedulable(self: *Vm, pid: Pid) !void {
        try self.enqueueDirect(pid);
    }

    /// Public drain for engines layered on the VM (M14 distribution tests).
    pub fn drainSignalsPub(self: *Vm, pid: Pid) !void {
        return self.drainSignals(pid);
    }

    /// Drain B's signal queue in delivery order — the ONLY place signals
    /// become observable to the process (erts: handled at execution points).
    /// e48-smp-s2: free a discarded signal's fragment (if any) — called on
    /// every path that drops a SigMsg without processing it (the mid-drain
    /// death sweep + Vm.deinit's undrained queues). Zero-leak invariant.
    fn freeSigFragment(self: *Vm, sm: *const SigMsg) void {
        switch (sm.sig) {
            .message_frag => |frag| {
                frag.ctx.deinit();
                self.gpa.destroy(frag);
            },
            // N8: exit/DOWN reasons are fragment-carried too — same zero-leak sweep.
            .exit_sig => |x| {
                x.rfrag.ctx.deinit();
                self.gpa.destroy(x.rfrag);
            },
            .down => |d| {
                d.rfrag.ctx.deinit();
                self.gpa.destroy(d.rfrag);
            },
            else => {},
        }
    }

    /// N8: build a sender-owned reason FRAGMENT (mirrors the message-fragment
    /// copy boundary at the send site): the reason is copied into a fresh
    /// fragment heap — the target's ctx is NEVER touched by the sender.
    pub fn reasonFrag(self: *Vm, src_ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx, reason: FinalTerms.Term) !*Fragment {
        const frag = self.gpa.create(Fragment) catch return error.OutOfMemory;
        errdefer self.gpa.destroy(frag);
        frag.ctx = FinalTerms.Ctx.init(self.gpa, self.atoms);
        errdefer frag.ctx.deinit();
        frag.root = try FinalTerms.gcCopy(&frag.ctx, src_ctx, reason);
        frag.prio = false;
        return frag;
    }

    pub fn drainSignals(self: *Vm, pid: Pid) !void {
        // ── S-epoch Option B (the sigq de-globalization, DIVERGENCE 614) ──
        // With `tl_drain_lv == null` (EVERY caller except `SmpEngine.worker`
        // SECTION 2 — the interpret-path drains run under an already-held `L_v`;
        // `driveModel`/`grant` are single-threaded) this function is
        // OBSERVATIONALLY IDENTICAL to the pre-B shape: same arms, same order,
        // same cleanup. SECTION 2 sets `tl_drain_lv` and the drain then runs
        // OFF `L_v`: (1) briefly take `L_v` for the `procs`-array deref (the
        // array reallocs on spawn under `L_v`; the `Process` BOX is heap-stable),
        // (2) DETACH the whole queue under the `L_sigq` leaf (senders append
        // under the same leaf — no torn vector), (3) release `L_v` and deliver
        // the owner-exclusive message bulk lock-free, (4) RE-escalate under
        // `L_v` only for the arms that reach `L_v`-domain state: `.exit_sig`
        // (terminate → cross-process cascade), `.down`/`.link_req`/`.unlink_req`
        // (they mutate `links`/`monitors_held`/`aliases`, which `resolveAliasOwner`
        // and `process_info` read under `L_v`), and the emitTrace→signal
        // sub-path (a `procs` deref). Signals appended DURING the detached
        // drain land in the fresh `sigq` and wake the proc through `signal`'s
        // `L_c` wake path — delivered on the next grant, per-pair stamp order
        // intact (their stamps are strictly later than every batch entry's).
        // N4 (DIVERGENCE 618): the deref rides the STABLE index — lock-free —
        // so the relocated drain takes NO `L_v` at its top at all: the box is
        // pointer-stable, `alive` is owner-exclusive for the granted pid, and
        // the detach below is `L_sigq`-guarded against every sender append.
        // `L_v` now appears ONLY inside the escalating arms. (With the detach
        // off `L_v`, append-vs-detach exclusion rests ENTIRELY on the leaf:
        // MUT-SIGQ-4 is hereby LIVE — re-verified under TSan this slice.)
        const p = blk: {
            const bp = self.stable_procs.get(pid) orelse {
                tl_drain_vp = null;
                return;
            };
            if (!bp.alive) {
                tl_drain_vp = null;
                return;
            }
            break :blk bp;
        };
        tl_drain_vp = p; // SECTION 2 reuses this deref
        var batch = blk: {
            p.sigq_lock.lock();
            defer p.sigq_lock.unlock();
            const b = p.sigq;
            p.sigq = .empty;
            break :blk b;
        };
        // TEST-ONLY yield-widen (skills/smp-concurrency-slice §3b, the gap-b-1
        // pattern): stretch the detach→deliver window so an append-vs-detach or
        // stranded-signal bug is RELIABLY reachable by the conservation law and
        // ThreadSanitizer — not a 1-in-a-million interleaving flake. Compiled
        // out of production builds (`is_test`), and only the RELOCATED drain
        // (SECTION 2) widens — under-`L_v` callers are unchanged.
        if (comptime @import("builtin").is_test) {
            if (tl_drain_lv != null) std.Thread.yield() catch {};
        }
        var i: usize = 0;
        // Zero-leak on EVERY exit (normal, mid-drain death, error): entries not
        // yet processed (index i onward) may carry fragments — free them, then
        // free the detached batch vector itself. (`.message_frag` overwrites its
        // entry after consuming the fragment, so this can never double-free.)
        defer {
            var j = i;
            while (j < batch.items.len) : (j += 1) self.freeSigFragment(&batch.items[j]);
            batch.deinit(self.gpa);
        }
        while (i < batch.items.len and p.alive) : (i += 1) {
            const sm = batch.items[i];
            const m = &p.machine;
            switch (sm.sig) {
                .message => |payload| {
                    try m.mbox.deliver(sm.from, payload);
                    try self.emitEvent(.{ .deliver = .{
                        .from = sm.from,
                        .to = pid,
                        .stamp = sm.stamp,
                        .vhash = FinalTerms.hashTerm(&m.ctx, payload),
                    } });
                    // e50-tracing: a 'receive' trace fires when a TRACED proc
                    // takes a message into its mailbox → {trace, Pid, receive, Msg}.
                    if (p.trace_recv) {
                        if (p.tracer) |tr| {
                            // Option B: emitTrace→signalTrace derefs `procs` —
                            // `L_v`-domain, so the relocated drain escalates.
                            if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.lock();
                            defer if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.unlock();
                            try self.emitTrace(tr, pid, "receive", &m.ctx, payload, null, null);
                        }
                    }
                },
                .message_frag => |frag| {
                    // e48-smp-s2: the RECEIVER-side merge — copy out of the
                    // fragment into this heap, free the fragment, deliver.
                    // Observationally identical to `.message` (same stamp, and
                    // the vhash is computed over the merged receiver-heap copy
                    // exactly as the direct path computed it).
                    const payload = try FinalTerms.gcCopy(&m.ctx, &frag.ctx, frag.root);
                    const was_prio = frag.prio;
                    frag.ctx.deinit();
                    self.gpa.destroy(frag);
                    // Option B: the fragment is CONSUMED — overwrite the entry so
                    // the cleanup `defer` (which frees frags from index i onward
                    // if a `try` below fails) can never free it twice.
                    batch.items[i].sig = .{ .message = payload };
                    // e50-eep76: a priority-alias message jumps to the front of
                    // the mailbox; every other send keeps arrival order.
                    if (was_prio) try m.mbox.deliverPrio(sm.from, payload) else try m.mbox.deliver(sm.from, payload);
                    try self.emitEvent(.{ .deliver = .{
                        .from = sm.from,
                        .to = pid,
                        .stamp = sm.stamp,
                        .vhash = FinalTerms.hashTerm(&m.ctx, payload),
                    } });
                    if (p.trace_recv) {
                        if (p.tracer) |tr| {
                            if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.lock();
                            defer if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.unlock();
                            try self.emitTrace(tr, pid, "receive", &m.ctx, payload, null, null);
                        }
                    }
                },
                .exit_sig => |x| {
                    // Option B ESCALATION: `terminate` reaches cross-process
                    // state (procs derefs, `signal`→`L_c`, registry, ETS, timers)
                    // that REQUIRES `L_v` as the outer lock. `defer` runs on
                    // every prong exit (`try` error, the kill-path `continue`,
                    // normal fall-off) — the lock never leaks.
                    if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.lock();
                    defer if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.unlock();
                    // e32-t1 fix (erts SIGKILL semantics): the direct exit/2 kill
                    // signal is UNTRAPPABLE — it terminates the victim (even a
                    // system process, even a trapping one) with the reason RENAMED
                    // to `killed` (fleet finding: c_exit2_kill expected DOWN
                    // `killed`, got the trap/`kill` paths).
                    // N8: merge the fragment-carried reason into OWN heap
                    // (owner-side, safe), free the fragment, and OVERWRITE the
                    // batch entry so the cleanup defer can never re-free it.
                    const x_reason = try FinalTerms.gcCopy(&m.ctx, &x.rfrag.ctx, x.rfrag.root);
                    x.rfrag.ctx.deinit();
                    self.gpa.destroy(x.rfrag);
                    batch.items[i].sig = .link_req;
                    if (x.kill) {
                        try self.terminate(pid, FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, self.a_killed));
                        continue;
                    }
                    const is_normal = FinalTerms.eqlExact(&m.ctx, x_reason, FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, self.a_normal));
                    if (p.machine.trap_exit) {
                        // {'EXIT', FromPid, Reason} becomes an ordinary message.
                        // E4.1: FromPid is a real pid TERM (like the E3.8 'DOWN'
                        // 5-tuple), so a compiled `.beam`'s `receive {'EXIT', P,
                        // R}` binding the SPECIFIC child pid returned by
                        // spawn_link/spawn_opt matches — an int here would only
                        // match a wildcard.
                        const tup = try FinalTerms.tuple(&m.ctx, &.{
                            FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, self.a_exit),
                            try FinalTerms.pid(&m.ctx, sm.from, 0),
                            x_reason,
                        });
                        try m.mbox.deliver(sm.from, tup);
                        try self.emitEvent(.{ .deliver = .{
                            .from = sm.from,
                            .to = pid,
                            .stamp = sm.stamp,
                            .vhash = FinalTerms.hashTerm(&m.ctx, tup),
                        } });
                    } else if (!is_normal) {
                        // E4.3 (tightened by e32-t1): a SYSTEM process ABSORBS an
                        // untrapped abnormal exit signal delivered over a link —
                        // including a link-propagated reason `kill` (ordinary in
                        // erts); ONLY the direct SIGKILL (x.kill, handled above)
                        // stops a system process.
                        if (!p.system) try self.terminate(pid, x_reason);
                    } // untrapped normal exit signal (or absorbed by a system proc): ignored
                },
                .down => |d| {
                    // Option B ESCALATION: mutates `monitors_held`/`aliases`,
                    // which `resolveAliasOwner`/`sendTargetIsPriority` (every
                    // ref-send) and `process_info` read under `L_v`.
                    if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.lock();
                    defer if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.unlock();
                    // 'DOWN' fires only if the monitor is still held
                    var held = false;
                    for (p.monitors_held.items) |mh| {
                        if (mh.ref == d.ref) held = true;
                    }
                    // N8: merge the fragment-carried reason (owner-side), free
                    // the fragment, overwrite the entry (double-free guard) —
                    // BEFORE the held-check so a not-held DOWN cannot leak it.
                    const d_reason = try FinalTerms.gcCopy(&m.ctx, &d.rfrag.ctx, d.rfrag.root);
                    d.rfrag.ctx.deinit();
                    self.gpa.destroy(d.rfrag);
                    batch.items[i].sig = .link_req;
                    if (held) {
                        // E3.8-fix: {'DOWN', Ref, process, Pid, Reason} — the real
                        // BEAM 5-tuple shape (was a 4-tuple missing the `process`
                        // Type element, with a disguised small-int Ref).
                        const ref_term = try FinalTerms.ref(&m.ctx, refWordsForKey(d.ref));
                        const pid_term = try FinalTerms.pid(&m.ctx, sm.from, 0);
                        const tup = try FinalTerms.tuple(&m.ctx, &.{
                            FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, self.a_down),
                            ref_term,
                            FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, self.a_process),
                            pid_term,
                            d_reason,
                        });
                        try m.mbox.deliver(sm.from, tup);
                        try self.emitEvent(.{ .down_delivered = .{ .to = pid, .ref = d.ref } });
                        // ref is now spent
                        for (p.monitors_held.items, 0..) |mh, k| {
                            if (mh.ref == d.ref) {
                                _ = p.monitors_held.swapRemove(k);
                                break;
                            }
                        }
                        // E5.5: a monitor-alias with the (non-explicit_unalias)
                        // auto-retire mode is deactivated on 'DOWN' delivery —
                        // future `Ref ! Msg` is then dropped (DIVERGENCE entry 19).
                        for (p.aliases.items) |*a| {
                            if (a.key == d.ref and a.retire_on_down) a.active = false;
                        }
                    }
                },
                .link_req => {
                    // Option B ESCALATION: mutates `links`, read under `L_v`
                    // by `terminate`'s cascade and `process_info(links)`.
                    if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.lock();
                    defer if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.unlock();
                    var have = false;
                    for (p.links.items) |l| {
                        if (l == sm.from) have = true;
                    }
                    if (!have) try p.links.append(self.gpa, sm.from);
                },
                .unlink_req => {
                    // Option B ESCALATION: mutates `links` (see `.link_req`).
                    if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.lock();
                    defer if (tl_drain_lv) |lv| lv.unlock();
                    for (p.links.items, 0..) |l, k| {
                        if (l == sm.from) {
                            _ = p.links.swapRemove(k);
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                },
            }
        }
        // (the unprocessed-fragment sweep + batch free live in the `defer` above —
        // it runs on THIS normal exit and on every error exit alike.)
    }

    /// E24-T3 — the ETS owner-death HEIR lifecycle, corrected against OTP-30
    /// `give_away_to_heir` (`erl_db.c:4865`, invoked from the process-exit ETS
    /// cleanup @5079). This COMPLETES DIVERGENCE entry 89's
    /// `deferred-E5-ets-ownership` residual: E6.7 landed the ownership REASSIGN
    /// but — because `EtsRegistry.ownerDied` sees only tables, not the process
    /// table — silently dropped BOTH the heir-LIVENESS check AND the
    /// `{'ETS-TRANSFER', Tab, DeadOwner, HeirData}` notification OTP delivers.
    /// Discovered by the E24 hardening probe (heir gained ownership but its
    /// mailbox stayed empty).
    ///
    /// For every table the dying `pid` owns:
    ///   • heir set, distinct from the owner, AND a currently-LIVE local process
    ///     ⇒ transfer ownership to it AND deliver
    ///     `{'ETS-TRANSFER', Tab, DeadOwner, HeirData}`, built in the HEIR's heap
    ///     with `heir_data` copied out of the dying owner's still-live ctx
    ///     (`owner_ctx`) BEFORE that heap is reaped;
    ///   • otherwise (no heir, or the heir is dead/absent) ⇒ DELETE the table
    ///     (OTP: `!give_away_to_heir` ⇒ the table is deleted).
    /// Supersedes the message-less, liveness-blind `EtsRegistry.ownerDied` on the
    /// owner-death path. Iterates by index so an in-loop `drop` (which nulls the
    /// slot) is safe. `owner_ctx` is the dying process's `&machine.ctx`.
    fn etsOwnerDied(self: *Vm, pid: Pid, owner_ctx: *FinalTerms.Ctx) error{OutOfMemory}!void {
        var id: usize = 0;
        while (id < self.ets.tables.items.len) : (id += 1) {
            const t = if (self.ets.tables.items[id]) |*tp| tp else continue;
            if (t.owner != pid) continue;
            // A heir counts only if it is set, distinct from the dead owner, and
            // still a live process (OTP's `to_proc == NULL` / dead-pid guards).
            const heir_pid: ?Pid =
                if (t.heir != 0 and t.heir != pid) std.math.cast(Pid, t.heir) else null;
            const hp = if (heir_pid) |h| self.proc(h) else null;
            if (hp != null and hp.?.alive) {
                const h = heir_pid.?;
                const hm = &hp.?.machine.ctx;
                // Tab identifier: the name atom for a named table, else the
                // integer tid (unnamed tables are integer-referenced in BEAM).
                const tab_term = if (t.named)
                    FinalTerms.atom(hm, t.name)
                else
                    FinalTerms.int(hm, @intCast(id));
                const et = FinalTerms.atom(hm, self.atoms.intern("ETS-TRANSFER") catch unreachable);
                const from_o = try FinalTerms.pid(hm, pid, 0);
                // heir_data lives on the (still-alive) dying owner's heap; copy
                // it into the heir's heap. Unset ⇒ `undefined` (BEAM default).
                const gift_o = if (t.heir_data) |hd|
                    try FinalTerms.gcCopy(hm, owner_ctx, hd)
                else
                    FinalTerms.atom(hm, self.atoms.intern("undefined") catch unreachable);
                const msg = try FinalTerms.tuple(hm, &.{ et, tab_term, from_o, gift_o });
                t.owner = h;
                t.heir = 0;
                t.heir_data = null;
                try self.signal(pid, h, .{ .message = msg });
            } else {
                self.ets.drop(id); // heir-less OR dead-heir ⇒ delete (OTP-exact)
            }
        }
    }

    /// Process termination: notify links (exit signals) and monitors
    /// ('DOWN'), exactly once, through the same stamped signal path.
    pub fn terminate(self: *Vm, pid: Pid, reason: FinalTerms.Term) error{OutOfMemory}!void {
        const p = self.proc(pid) orelse return;
        p.alive = false;
        p.machine.status = .crashed;
        p.machine.result = reason;
        self.registry.pidExited(pid); // E3.8: DEATH unregisters (registry.zig law)
        try self.etsOwnerDied(pid, &p.machine.ctx); // E24-T3: heir liveness + ETS-TRANSFER (was E7.3 ets.ownerDied — see etsOwnerDied doc)
        try self.clearDistLocalForPid(pid); // E8.2s: local dist identity is net_kernel-owned
        try self.emitEvent(.{ .exited = .{
            .pid = pid,
            .rhash = FinalTerms.hashTerm(&p.machine.ctx, reason),
        } });
        // links: exit signals (copied into each target heap)
        for (p.links.items) |l| {
            if (self.proc(l)) |_| {
                try self.signal(pid, l, .{ .exit_sig = .{ .rfrag = try self.reasonFrag(&p.machine.ctx, reason) } });
            }
        }
        // monitors on me: 'DOWN' to each watcher
        for (p.monitored_by.items) |mb| {
            if (self.proc(mb.watcher)) |_| {
                try self.signal(pid, mb.watcher, .{ .down = .{ .ref = mb.ref, .rfrag = try self.reasonFrag(&p.machine.ctx, reason) } });
            }
        }
    }

    fn normalExit(self: *Vm, pid: Pid) !void {
        const p = self.proc(pid) orelse return;
        const reason = FinalTerms.atom(&p.machine.ctx, self.a_normal);
        // links get 'normal' (ignored unless trapping); monitors get 'DOWN'
        p.alive = false;
        self.registry.pidExited(pid); // E3.8: DEATH unregisters (registry.zig law)
        try self.etsOwnerDied(pid, &p.machine.ctx); // E24-T3: heir liveness + ETS-TRANSFER (was E7.3 ets.ownerDied — see etsOwnerDied doc)
        try self.clearDistLocalForPid(pid); // E8.2s: local dist identity is net_kernel-owned
        try self.emitEvent(.{ .exited = .{
            .pid = pid,
            .rhash = FinalTerms.hashTerm(&p.machine.ctx, reason),
        } });
        for (p.links.items) |l| {
            if (self.proc(l)) |_| {
                try self.signal(pid, l, .{ .exit_sig = .{ .rfrag = try self.reasonFrag(&p.machine.ctx, reason) } });
            }
        }
        for (p.monitored_by.items) |mb| {
            if (self.proc(mb.watcher)) |_| {
                try self.signal(pid, mb.watcher, .{ .down = .{ .ref = mb.ref, .rfrag = try self.reasonFrag(&p.machine.ctx, reason) } });
            }
        }
    }

    /// Interpret one trapped action for `pid`, then clear it.
    fn interpret(self: *Vm, pid: Pid) !void {
        const p = self.proc(pid) orelse return;
        const m = &p.machine;
        const act = m.pending orelse return;
        m.pending = null;
        switch (act) {
            .spawn => |v| {
                const child = try self.spawn(p.prog, v.to, null);
                self.procs.items[child].machine.group_leader = m.group_leader; // E3.8: inherit
                try self.emitEvent(.{ .spawned = .{ .parent = pid, .child = child } });
                // E3.7: the spawn RESULT is now a pid TERM naming the child (was a
                // small-int). `number` == the child's proc index — the pid↔process
                // mapping. Mutant 1 (return the PARENT's pid) is planted here.
                m.regs[v.dst] = try FinalTerms.pid(&m.ctx, child, 0);
            },
            .send_to => |v| {
                // E5.5: a REF destination routes to the owner of the active alias
                // it names (`resolveSendTarget`); a retired/unknown alias → drop.
                const to = self.resolveSendTarget(&m.ctx, v.pid) orelse return; // badarg-ish: drop
                if (self.proc(to)) |target| {
                    _ = target;
                    // e48-smp-s2 — THE COPY BOUNDARY, fragment edition: the
                    // SENDER copies into a fresh fragment heap (its own
                    // allocation; the receiver's ctx is NEVER touched here);
                    // the receiver merges on drain. Same denotation, same
                    // stamp order — the write dependency S3 must sever.
                    const frag = self.gpa.create(Fragment) catch return error.OutOfMemory;
                    errdefer self.gpa.destroy(frag);
                    frag.ctx = FinalTerms.Ctx.init(self.gpa, self.atoms);
                    errdefer frag.ctx.deinit();
                    frag.root = try FinalTerms.gcCopy(&frag.ctx, &m.ctx, v.msg);
                    // e50-eep76: a priority-alias destination routes to the
                    // FRONT-of-mailbox delivery path (drainSignals reads frag.prio).
                    frag.prio = self.sendTargetIsPriority(&m.ctx, v.pid);
                    try self.signal(pid, to, .{ .message_frag = frag });
                    // e50-tracing: a 'send' trace fires when a TRACED proc sends
                    // → {trace, From, send, Msg, To}.
                    if (p.trace_send) {
                        if (p.tracer) |tr| {
                            const to_pid = try FinalTerms.pid(&m.ctx, to, 0);
                            try self.emitTrace(tr, pid, "send", &m.ctx, v.msg, to_pid, &m.ctx);
                        }
                    }
                }
            },
            .link_to => |v| {
                const to = pidOfTerm(&m.ctx, v.pid) orelse return;
                if (self.proc(to) != null) {
                    var have = false;
                    for (p.links.items) |l| {
                        if (l == to) have = true;
                    }
                    if (!have) try p.links.append(self.gpa, to);
                    try self.signal(pid, to, .link_req);
                }
            },
            .unlink_to => |v| {
                const to = pidOfTerm(&m.ctx, v.pid) orelse return;
                for (p.links.items, 0..) |l, k| {
                    if (l == to) {
                        _ = p.links.swapRemove(k);
                        break;
                    }
                }
                try self.signal(pid, to, .unlink_req);
            },
            .monitor_to => |v| {
                const to = pidOfTerm(&m.ctx, v.pid) orelse return;
                const ref = self.next_ref;
                self.next_ref += 1;
                // E3.8-fix: erlang:monitor/2 returns a genuine boxed ref (E3.5),
                // not a disguised small int.
                m.regs[v.dst] = try FinalTerms.ref(&m.ctx, refWordsForKey(ref));
                // E5.5: `monitor(process, Pid, [{alias, Mode}])` mints an alias
                // sharing the monitor ref — the owner can `Ref ! Reply` and the
                // 'DOWN' retires it unless `explicit_unalias` (DIVERGENCE 19).
                if (v.alias != .none) try p.aliases.append(self.gpa, .{ .key = ref, .active = true, .retire_on_down = (v.alias == .retire) });
                if (self.proc(to)) |target| {
                    try p.monitors_held.append(self.gpa, .{ .ref = ref, .target = to });
                    try target.monitored_by.append(self.gpa, .{ .ref = ref, .watcher = pid });
                } else {
                    // monitoring a dead pid: immediate 'DOWN' (erts semantics)
                    try p.monitors_held.append(self.gpa, .{ .ref = ref, .target = to });
                    const reason = FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, try self.atoms.intern("noproc"));
                    try self.signal(to, pid, .{ .down = .{ .ref = ref, .rfrag = try self.reasonFrag(&m.ctx, reason) } });
                }
            },
            .demonitor_ref => |v| {
                // E3.8-fix: `demonitor(Ref)` now maps a REAL ref term → the
                // internal u64 key via its words (see `keyFromRefWords`).
                if (!FinalTerms.repIsRef(&m.ctx, v.ref)) return;
                const ref: u64 = keyFromRefWords(FinalTerms.refWords(&m.ctx, v.ref));
                var existed = false;
                for (p.monitors_held.items, 0..) |mh, k| {
                    if (mh.ref == ref) {
                        existed = true;
                        if (self.proc(mh.target)) |t| {
                            for (t.monitored_by.items, 0..) |mb, j| {
                                if (mb.ref == ref) {
                                    _ = t.monitored_by.swapRemove(j);
                                    break;
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        _ = p.monitors_held.swapRemove(k);
                        break;
                    }
                }
                if (v.info) {
                    m.regs[0] = FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, if (existed) m.bool_true else m.bool_false);
                } else {
                    m.regs[0] = FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, m.bool_true);
                }
            },
            .exit_proc => |v| try self.terminate(pid, v.reason),
            .trap_exits => |v| p.machine.trap_exit = v.on,
            // ---- E3.7: the process/system BIF traps (bifs/procsys.zig). Result
            // register is x0 (regs[0]) — the BEAM call-return convention.
            .spawn_fun => |v| {
                // spawn/1, spawn_link/1: run the fun `F()` on a fresh Machine at
                // F's label. The child shares `p.prog` (single-module scope); F's
                // captured env is copied CROSS-HEAP into the child's low x-regs
                // (the M1 copy law at the spawn boundary). Returns the CHILD pid.
                const label: u16 = @intCast(FinalTerms.funLabel(&m.ctx, v.fun));
                const child = try self.spawn(p.prog, label, null);
                self.procs.items[child].machine.group_leader = m.group_leader; // E3.8: inherit
                try self.inheritCode(child, m); // E4.1: child sees the linked code space
                try self.emitEvent(.{ .spawned = .{ .parent = pid, .child = child } });
                const cp = self.procs.items[child];
                const env_len = FinalTerms.funParts(&m.ctx, v.fun).env_len;
                var k: usize = 0;
                while (k < env_len and k < 16) : (k += 1) {
                    cp.machine.regs[k] = try FinalTerms.gcCopy(&cp.machine.ctx, &m.ctx, FinalTerms.funEnvElem(&m.ctx, v.fun, k));
                }
                if (v.link) {
                    try p.links.append(self.gpa, child);
                    try self.signal(pid, child, .link_req);
                }
                // E5.8 (Task 8): `spawn_monitor/1` returns `{Pid, Ref}`.
                m.regs[0] = try self.spawnResult(p, m, pid, child, v.monitor);
            },
            .spawn_mfa => |v| {
                // spawn/3, spawn_link/3, spawn_opt/4. E4.1: MFA→entry resolution
                // over the LIVE export index (the E3.12b linker's `m.exports`,
                // inherited by the child): a resolvable local `M:F/A` runs its
                // function with the arg list unpacked (cross-heap copied) into the
                // child's x0..x[A-1]; an UNRESOLVABLE MFA spawns a process that
                // immediately exits `undef` (BEAM-faithful). The parent's link (if
                // any) is installed BEFORE the child runs so an abnormal exit
                // propagates. No leak: the child is a normal Process, reaped
                // through the standard terminate path.
                const child = try self.spawn(p.prog, 0, null);
                const cp = self.procs.items[child];
                cp.machine.group_leader = m.group_leader; // E3.8: inherit
                try self.inheritCode(child, m);
                try self.emitEvent(.{ .spawned = .{ .parent = pid, .child = child } });
                if (v.link) {
                    try p.links.append(self.gpa, child);
                    try self.signal(pid, child, .link_req);
                }
                // E5.8 (Task 8): a `[monitor]` spawn (`spawn_opt`/`spawn_monitor/3`)
                // returns `{Pid, Ref}` and installs the monitor BEFORE the child
                // runs, so an unresolvable-MFA `undef` exit fires the 'DOWN'.
                m.regs[0] = try self.spawnResult(p, m, pid, child, v.monitor);
                if (self.resolveSpawnMFA(cp, m, v.module, v.func, v.args)) |pc| {
                    cp.machine.pc = pc;
                } else {
                    const undef = FinalTerms.atom(&cp.machine.ctx, try self.atoms.intern("undef"));
                    try self.terminate(child, undef);
                }
            },
            .spawn_system => |v| {
                // E4.3: erts_internal:spawn_system_process/3. Like spawn_mfa but the
                // child is a SYSTEM process (`Process.system = true`) and is NOT
                // linked to the spawner — a supervision-tree crash cannot cascade
                // into the kernel bring-up set. MFA→entry resolution reuses the E4.1
                // export-index machinery; an unresolvable MFA exits `undef`. Returns
                // the child pid in x0.
                const child = try self.spawn(p.prog, 0, null);
                const cp = self.procs.items[child];
                cp.system = true;
                cp.machine.group_leader = m.group_leader; // inherit
                try self.inheritCode(child, m);
                try self.emitEvent(.{ .spawned = .{ .parent = pid, .child = child } });
                m.regs[0] = try FinalTerms.pid(&m.ctx, child, 0);
                if (self.resolveSpawnMFA(cp, m, v.module, v.func, v.args)) |pc| {
                    cp.machine.pc = pc;
                } else {
                    const undef = FinalTerms.atom(&cp.machine.ctx, try self.atoms.intern("undef"));
                    try self.terminate(child, undef);
                }
            },
            .run_on_load => |v| {
                // E7.2 (DIVERGENCE 147): `call_on_load_function/1` runs the staged
                // module's on_load function in the CALLING process. `m.pc` was
                // advanced past the trapping call_ext, so it IS the continuation:
                // push it and jump to the on_load entry. When on_load `ret`s, the
                // continuation pops and x0 carries its result (the BIF's value) —
                // the SACRED M7 per-pair signal order is untouched (no signal is
                // sent; this is a local call/return over `m.stack`).
                try m.stack.append(self.gpa, m.pc);
                m.pc = v.entry;
            },
            .exit_to => |v| {
                // exit/2 and exit_signal/2,3: send an EXIT signal to a local pid or
                // active local alias. Foreign pid/ref identities have no carrier here
                // and are dropped, never remapped by numeric coincidence.
                const to = self.resolveExitTarget(&m.ctx, v.pid) orelse return;
                if (self.proc(to)) |_| {
                    // e32-t1 fix: the DIRECT exit/2 kill is the untrappable SIGKILL.
                    const is_kill = FinalTerms.eqlExact(&m.ctx, v.reason, FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, try self.atoms.intern("kill")));
                    try self.signal(pid, to, .{ .exit_sig = .{ .rfrag = try self.reasonFrag(&m.ctx, v.reason), .kill = is_kill } });
                }
            },
            .is_alive => |v| {
                // is_process_alive/1 → true iff the pid names a live process.
                const to = pidOfTerm(&m.ctx, v.pid);
                const alive = to != null and self.proc(to.?) != null;
                const idx = try self.atoms.intern(if (alive) "true" else "false");
                m.regs[0] = FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, idx);
            },
            .list_processes => {
                // processes/0 → the list of live local pids (ascending index).
                var list = FinalTerms.nil(&m.ctx);
                var i = self.procs.items.len;
                while (i > 0) {
                    i -= 1;
                    if (self.procs.items[i].alive) {
                        const pt = try FinalTerms.pid(&m.ctx, @intCast(i), 0);
                        list = try FinalTerms.cons(&m.ctx, pt, list);
                    }
                }
                m.regs[0] = list;
            },
            // ---- E3.8: link/monitor/registry BIF-family traps ----
            .register_name => |v| {
                // gap-erlang-cover-registry (DIVERGENCE 658): a rejected register
                // RAISES a catchable `error:badarg`, never returns `false`.
                m.regs[0] = self.doRegister(m, pid, v.name, v.pid) catch |err| switch (err) {
                    error.Badarg => {
                        try m.raiseErrorAtom(m.badarg);
                        return;
                    },
                    else => return err,
                };
            },
            .unregister_name => |v| {
                m.regs[0] = self.doUnregister(m, v.name) catch |err| switch (err) {
                    error.Badarg => {
                        try m.raiseErrorAtom(m.badarg);
                        return;
                    },
                    else => return err,
                };
            },
            .whereis_name => |v| {
                m.regs[0] = try self.doWhereis(m, v.name);
            },
            .list_registered => {
                m.regs[0] = try self.doListRegistered(m);
            },
            .stat_run_queue => {
                // gap-real-metrics (FM-OBS-1): the REAL runnable-process count —
                // `Vm.liveCount()` (schedulable procs), not the old fabricated 0.
                m.regs[0] = FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, @intCast(self.liveCount()));
            },
            .stat_memory => |kind| {
                // gap-real-metrics (DIVERGENCE 627, FM-OBS-1): TRUTHFUL measured
                // bytes — `processes` = Σ each live proc's tagged-word heap
                // (words.items.len * 8, the EXACT zigvm heap), `atom` = Σ interned
                // name bytes, `total` = their sum. BEAM-arena categories
                // (code/system/binary/ets) are OMITTED from /0 and badarg on /1 —
                // NEVER a fabricated value. Values are host-nondeterministic
                // (property-verified, not byte-EQ), like statistics/1.
                var proc_bytes: u64 = 0;
                for (self.procs.items) |pp| {
                    if (pp.alive) proc_bytes += @as(u64, pp.machine.ctx.words.items.len) * 8;
                }
                var atom_bytes: u64 = 0;
                var ai: usize = 0;
                const acount = self.atoms.count();
                while (ai < acount) : (ai += 1) atom_bytes += self.atoms.nameOf(@intCast(ai)).len;
                const total = proc_bytes + atom_bytes;
                m.regs[0] = switch (kind) {
                    .total => FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, @intCast(total)),
                    .processes => FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, @intCast(proc_bytes)),
                    .atom => FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, @intCast(atom_bytes)),
                    .all => blk: {
                        // {total,T},{processes,P},{atom,A} — the truthfully-measured
                        // subset (a SHORTER proplist than OTP's, the disclosed bound).
                        const k_total = FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, try self.atoms.intern("total"));
                        const k_proc = FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, try self.atoms.intern("processes"));
                        const k_atom = FinalTerms.atom(&m.ctx, try self.atoms.intern("atom"));
                        const t3 = try FinalTerms.tuple(&m.ctx, &.{ k_total, FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, @intCast(total)) });
                        const p3 = try FinalTerms.tuple(&m.ctx, &.{ k_proc, FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, @intCast(proc_bytes)) });
                        const a3 = try FinalTerms.tuple(&m.ctx, &.{ k_atom, FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, @intCast(atom_bytes)) });
                        var l = FinalTerms.nil(&m.ctx);
                        l = try FinalTerms.cons(&m.ctx, a3, l);
                        l = try FinalTerms.cons(&m.ctx, p3, l);
                        l = try FinalTerms.cons(&m.ctx, t3, l);
                        break :blk l;
                    },
                };
            },
            .os_cmd => |v| {
                // e49-real-ports-io: os:cmd/1 over the proven live-port seam —
                // fork+exec /bin/sh -c CMD, EOF the stdin, drain stdout to EOF
                // (BOUNDED: 1 MiB cap + idle fuel — a silent child is error,
                // never a hang), reap the child, return stdout as a charlist.
                const bin = FinalTerms.iolistToBinary(&m.ctx, v.cmd) catch {
                    m.regs[0] = self.badargAtom(m);
                    return;
                };
                const bytes = FinalTerms.binBytes(&m.ctx, bin);
                if (bytes.len == 0 or bytes.len >= 512) {
                    m.regs[0] = self.badargAtom(m);
                    return;
                }
                var cbuf: [512]u8 = undefined;
                @memcpy(cbuf[0..bytes.len], bytes);
                cbuf[bytes.len] = 0;
                const cmdz: [*:0]const u8 = @ptrCast(&cbuf);
                var live = os_port.LivePort.spawnShell(cmdz) catch {
                    m.regs[0] = self.badargAtom(m);
                    return;
                };
                defer live.deinit(); // reap on every path (no fd/child leak)
                live.closeStdin();
                const out_bytes = live.readToEof(self.gpa, 1 << 20, 400) catch {
                    m.regs[0] = self.badargAtom(m);
                    return;
                };
                defer self.gpa.free(out_bytes);
                // charlist result (os:cmd returns a string)
                var lst = FinalTerms.nil(&m.ctx);
                var i = out_bytes.len;
                while (i > 0) {
                    i -= 1;
                    lst = try FinalTerms.cons(&m.ctx, FinalTerms.int(&m.ctx, out_bytes[i]), lst);
                }
                m.regs[0] = lst;
            },
            .file_read => |v| {
